WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.955 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.955 --> 00:00:03.895 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.895 --> 00:00:05.835 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.835 --> 00:00:09.785 align:middle line:90%


00:00:09.785 --> 00:00:11.160 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So today
I want to talk

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.240 align:middle line:84%
about-- we're going to
move to the final phase

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.820 align:middle line:84%
of the course, which is going
to be on dynamic Bayesian games.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.980 align:middle line:84%
But I want to start with maybe
one example of a VCG mechanism,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.800 align:middle line:84%
because I think,
yesterday, that was maybe

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.467 align:middle line:84%
a bit abstract and a
bit hard to follow.

00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:26.020 align:middle line:84%
So let's start with
one final example.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.580 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to
be a VCG mechanism,

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:32.580 align:middle line:84%
or maybe we'll call it a
VCG auction for two items.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:40.520 align:middle line:90%


00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.738 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of the classical
work that we did so far

00:00:42.738 --> 00:00:45.280 align:middle line:84%
in the course on auctions, we
assumed there was just one item

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.392 align:middle line:90%
being auctioned off.

00:00:46.392 --> 00:00:48.100 align:middle line:84%
Then we looked at the
ad auction setting,

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.160 align:middle line:84%
which had this special
structure where there was

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.457 align:middle line:90%
this ranking between the items.

00:00:51.457 --> 00:00:53.540 align:middle line:84%
Here, we're just going to
have two abstract items.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:01:00.470 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to call these
maybe item A and item B.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.970 align:middle line:84%
So what the auction needs to
do is it needs to solicit bids.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.630 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll write what
it's going to do.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:12.450 align:middle line:84%
It's going to collect
bids from the bidders.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:17.030 align:middle line:90%


00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.210 align:middle line:84%
And then, based upon
these bids, as usual,

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.710 align:middle line:90%
we have to decide two things.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.870 align:middle line:84%
We have to decide how
we allocate the goods

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.750 align:middle line:90%
and how much everyone pays.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:30.250 align:middle line:84%
So then it specifies an
allocation, which, in this case,

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:33.270 align:middle line:84%
it says who gets item
A if someone gets it

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.423 align:middle line:84%
and who gets item B
if anyone gets it.

00:01:36.423 --> 00:01:38.090 align:middle line:84%
And then, in addition
to the allocation,

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.050 align:middle line:84%
we have to specify a
payment by each bidder.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:46.950 align:middle line:90%


00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.030 align:middle line:84%
Now, I put bids in
quotes because the kind

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.750 align:middle line:84%
of surprising or maybe
somewhat confusing

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.870 align:middle line:84%
aspect of a VCG auction is
that a bid is essentially

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.590 align:middle line:84%
a report or claim about how
much you value the items.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.850 align:middle line:84%
So you think of my bid
as the auctioneer saying,

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.450 align:middle line:84%
tell me how much you
value everything.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.808 align:middle line:84%
And then the bidders say,
I value it this much.

00:02:07.808 --> 00:02:09.350 align:middle line:84%
And that may seem
strange, but if you

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.009 align:middle line:84%
think about a
second-price auction,

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:12.230 align:middle line:84%
that's exactly what
happens because,

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.830 align:middle line:84%
in a second-price
auction, people are

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.265 align:middle line:90%
bidding their true valuation.

00:02:15.265 --> 00:02:17.890 align:middle line:84%
So it's as if the auctioneer is
saying, tell me your valuation.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.590 align:middle line:84%
And then the bidders are
submitting their valuation.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:25.590 align:middle line:84%
So let's put a little
more structure on this.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.130 align:middle line:84%
So let's imagine that
we have two bidders.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:31.910 align:middle line:90%


00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.390 align:middle line:84%
And it's a little complicated
to specify the whole auction

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.110 align:middle line:84%
because we have to
specify the allocation

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:38.588 align:middle line:84%
and payment for any vector
of bids that's submitted.

00:02:38.588 --> 00:02:40.130 align:middle line:84%
So let's just go
through one example.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.950 align:middle line:84%
Let's look at some vector of
bids that could be submitted.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.508 align:middle line:84%
So, well, we first have to
say, well, what is a valuation?

00:02:46.508 --> 00:02:48.050 align:middle line:84%
What do I need to
tell the auctioneer

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.290 align:middle line:84%
if the auctioneer is asking
me how much I value things?

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.040 align:middle line:84%
Well, I actually have
to say three things.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.390 align:middle line:84%
I have to say, how much do
I value item A if I just

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:55.820 align:middle line:90%
get item A?

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.580 align:middle line:84%
How much do I value item
B If I just get item B?

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.300 align:middle line:84%
And then how much do I value
getting both items together?

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.260 align:middle line:84%
And depending on these
items, it may not

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:06.700 align:middle line:84%
be the value of the
bundle of the two items

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.300 align:middle line:84%
is exactly the same as some
of your valuations for the two

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.860 align:middle line:90%
items.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.340 align:middle line:84%
If it's shoes, a left
shoe and a right shoe

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.900 align:middle line:84%
together are much more
valuable than the two shoes

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.540 align:middle line:84%
separately whereas other items
that are substitutes, the bundle

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:23.717 align:middle line:84%
may be less valuable than
the sum of its parts,

00:03:23.717 --> 00:03:25.800 align:middle line:84%
if there's some duplication
between the two parts.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.580 align:middle line:84%
Maybe 2 apples is not twice
as valuable as 1 apple.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:36.140 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to think
of it as a table where

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:39.120 align:middle line:84%
my valuation is going to
say, if I get item A alone,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.000 align:middle line:90%
what's my value?

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.080 align:middle line:84%
If I get item B alone,
what's my value?

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.180 align:middle line:84%
And if I get the
bundle, what's my value?

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.160 align:middle line:84%
So bidder 1 is going
to make some report.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.460 align:middle line:84%
And bidder 2 is going
to make some report.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:51.540 align:middle line:84%
I've made the numbers
here so it works out.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.170 align:middle line:84%
So let's suppose that the
report beta 1 makes is they say,

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:59.330 align:middle line:84%
if I get only item
A, I value that at 5.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:03.890 align:middle line:84%
If I get only item B, I valued
at 3, and if I get both items,

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:05.490 align:middle line:90%
I valued at 8.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:09.050 align:middle line:84%
So for bidder 1, it turns
out, their value of the bundle

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.550 align:middle line:84%
is exactly the sum of the
value of the two parts,

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.690 align:middle line:84%
but it doesn't have
to be that way.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:16.450 align:middle line:90%
Now let's look at bidder 2.

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:20.649 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose bidder 2 says,
I value item A alone at 2.

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:24.610 align:middle line:84%
I value B alone at 6, but I
only value the bundle at 7.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:26.970 align:middle line:84%
So notice for
bidder 2, the bundle

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:30.150 align:middle line:84%
is not worth as much to them
as the sum of its parts.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.210 align:middle line:84%
If they just got
A and if they just

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.710 align:middle line:84%
got B, that's different from
if they get the bundle together

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.867 align:middle line:84%
because, for them, these two
items, maybe if item B is

00:04:38.867 --> 00:04:41.450 align:middle line:84%
a better version of item A, then
getting both of them together

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:44.250 align:middle line:84%
is basically just as
good as getting item B.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.410 align:middle line:84%
So let's suppose these are the
reported valuations for the two

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:49.170 align:middle line:90%
bidders.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:52.620 align:middle line:84%
And now we have to specify who
gets and what the payments are.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.000 align:middle line:90%
So let's go through this.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.620 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we'll do
allocation over here.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:00.080 align:middle line:90%


00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.640 align:middle line:84%
So remember, the rule
of the VCG mechanism

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.040 align:middle line:84%
is that we always allocate
things efficiently.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.520 align:middle line:84%
So that is, we want
to maximize the sum

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:09.480 align:middle line:90%
of the players' utilities.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:14.120 align:middle line:84%
So we need to think,
who should item A go to?

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.623 align:middle line:90%
And who should I be go to?

00:05:15.623 --> 00:05:17.040 align:middle line:84%
Any thoughts here
on what would be

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.633 align:middle line:84%
the efficient way of allocating
things, given these reports?

00:05:20.633 --> 00:05:22.800 align:middle line:84%
Remember, when we say we
allocated efficiently, what

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.080 align:middle line:84%
we mean is, we
collect the reports,

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.960 align:middle line:84%
and we allocate in a way that
would be efficient if these were

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.920 align:middle line:90%
the players true valuations.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:31.920 align:middle line:84%
So taking this as the
truth, let's think

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.600 align:middle line:84%
about the efficient
way of allocating it?

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.900 align:middle line:90%
So how would you do this?

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:38.800 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.160 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You could
[INAUDIBLE] person 1.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.767 align:middle line:90%


00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:46.100 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right, that seems
like a good way of doing it.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:46.840 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.440 align:middle line:84%
If we give item A to person
1, they get a valuation of 5.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.040 align:middle line:84%
If we give item B
to person 2, they

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.980 align:middle line:84%
get evaluation of 6,
which means the sum is 11.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:57.980 align:middle line:90%
That sounds pretty good.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.920 align:middle line:84%
If we did the opposite, we
would only get a sum of 5.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:02.860 align:middle line:84%
And if we gave both
items to the same player,

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:04.140 align:middle line:90%
we'd either get 8 or 7.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:05.540 align:middle line:90%
But that's still less than 11.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.180 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be
the efficient allocation.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:08.805 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be
the allocation that

00:06:08.805 --> 00:06:12.660 align:middle line:84%
maximizes the sum of
the reported utilities.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.400 align:middle line:84%
But now we have to
do the payments.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:18.460 align:middle line:84%
And I think the payments
part is a bit trickier.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:22.530 align:middle line:84%
So we have to specify
two payments, t1 and t2.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.280 align:middle line:84%
And maybe if I'm more
precise, I would say

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:28.400 align:middle line:90%
this is t1 of v1 hat, v2 hat.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.920 align:middle line:84%
And this is t2 of
v1 hat, v2 hat.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.040 align:middle line:84%
So what the mechanism is saying
is if these are the submitted

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.900 align:middle line:84%
valuation reports that
the two players submit,

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:40.163 align:middle line:84%
this is how much I'm going
to ask player 1 to pay.

00:06:40.163 --> 00:06:42.580 align:middle line:84%
And if these are the valuation
reports that are submitted,

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:44.935 align:middle line:84%
this is how much I'm going
to ask player 2 to pay.

00:06:44.935 --> 00:06:46.560 align:middle line:84%
And the full mechanism
is going to have

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.470 align:middle line:84%
to specify a function that
says what these payments are

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.270 align:middle line:84%
for any submitted
reports, but writing out

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:52.650 align:middle line:84%
that full function's
a bit complicated.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:53.950 align:middle line:90%
So we'll just do this example.

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:57.510 align:middle line:84%
Now, what we said
in class last time

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:01.870 align:middle line:84%
is what bidder 1 is supposed to
pay is the externality that they

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.495 align:middle line:90%
impose on the other bidders.

00:07:03.495 --> 00:07:04.870 align:middle line:84%
So we have to
compute two things.

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:08.210 align:middle line:84%
We have to say if we just
cared about the other bidders

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:09.990 align:middle line:84%
and we allocated the
items to maximize

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:12.990 align:middle line:84%
the utility of the other
bidders, how well would they do?

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:14.710 align:middle line:84%
And then we have to
subtract from that

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:17.650 align:middle line:84%
how well the bidders actually
do under this allocation.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:20.110 align:middle line:84%
So let's start with
the first part.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:22.930 align:middle line:84%
And this is how well the
other bidders could do.

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:24.910 align:middle line:90%
So let's say I'm bidder 1.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.150 align:middle line:84%
These are the
submitted valuations.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.870 align:middle line:84%
If we gave bidder 2, if we just
allocated things in the best way

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:35.310 align:middle line:84%
for bidder 2, what would
bidder 2's valuation be?

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:36.750 align:middle line:84%
Well, what we would
do is we would

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.270 align:middle line:90%
give both items to bidder 2.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.350 align:middle line:84%
And if we gave both
items to bidder 2,

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:43.530 align:middle line:84%
then bidder 2's
valuation would be 7.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.910 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
first start with a 7

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:49.020 align:middle line:84%
here because this is how
well the other bidders could

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.657 align:middle line:84%
do optimally if bidder 1
weren't here, or in other words,

00:07:51.657 --> 00:07:53.740 align:middle line:84%
if we didn't care about
bidder 1 and we only tried

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:56.100 align:middle line:90%
to maximize bidder 2's payoff.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:08:00.980 align:middle line:84%
Now we want to subtract from
that how well bidder 2 actually

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:02.020 align:middle line:90%
does.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:04.840 align:middle line:84%
How well does bidder 2 actually
do under the given allocation?

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.020 align:middle line:90%
What's their actual valuation?

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.100 align:middle line:90%
6, right?

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:11.500 align:middle line:90%
So we subtract 6, and we get 1.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:15.220 align:middle line:84%
So this is the payment that
bidder 1 is asked to make.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:16.360 align:middle line:90%
Why is it 1?

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:20.140 align:middle line:84%
Well, because what is
the effect that they're

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:21.520 align:middle line:90%
having on the other bidders?

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.020 align:middle line:84%
The other bidder, bidder 2,
could've gotten the bundle A,

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:26.900 align:middle line:84%
B, but because of the
presence of bidder 1,

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.820 align:middle line:84%
bidder 2 is only getting
the item B by itself.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:33.700 align:middle line:84%
And the difference in utility
for bidder 2, between the bundle

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.880 align:middle line:84%
and getting only B by
itself is exactly 1.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:38.179 align:middle line:90%
And that's why we get this.

00:08:38.179 --> 00:08:40.659 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's do it for bidder 2.

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:42.700 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm bidder
2, we first need

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:44.460 align:middle line:84%
to compute how
well bidder 1 would

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.410 align:middle line:90%
do if bidder 2 weren't here.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:49.810 align:middle line:84%
If bidder 2 weren't here,
bidder 1 would get everything,

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.550 align:middle line:90%
and their utility would be 8.

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.650 align:middle line:90%


00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:57.090 align:middle line:84%
How well does bidder
1 actually do?

00:08:57.090 --> 00:09:00.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, bidder 1 actually
gets item A, which

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:02.890 align:middle line:90%
gives them a utility of 5.

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:05.250 align:middle line:90%
So we get 8 minus 5.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:08.090 align:middle line:90%
And we see the payment is 3.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.250 align:middle line:84%
Why does bidder 2
have to pay more?

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:13.850 align:middle line:84%
Because bidder 2 has a bigger
externality on bidder 1.

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:17.930 align:middle line:84%
Bidder 1 would've been able to
get everything together A and B,

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:19.890 align:middle line:84%
but because of the
presence of bidder 2,

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.370 align:middle line:84%
they only get item A by
itself, and the difference

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:26.268 align:middle line:84%
in utility between 8 and 5 is
3, which is a larger number.

00:09:26.268 --> 00:09:27.810 align:middle line:84%
And this is going
to-- this completes

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:31.790 align:middle line:84%
the specification of VCG
for this particular profile

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:32.690 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:34.658 align:middle line:84%
And the result
that I didn't prove

00:09:34.658 --> 00:09:36.450 align:middle line:84%
but that I claimed
yesterday is that if you

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.930 align:middle line:84%
use this kind of rule,
in a VCG auction,

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:41.330 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be
optimal for each bidder

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:42.550 align:middle line:90%
to report truthfully.

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:46.050 align:middle line:84%
So I should maybe
make a note on this.

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:56.170 align:middle line:84%
There's an optimal
strategy for each bidder.

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:57.350 align:middle line:90%
And what is that strategy?

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:58.710 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's reporting truthfully.

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:04.450 align:middle line:90%


00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:09.010 align:middle line:84%
And we might call
that vi hat equals vi.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:10.642 align:middle line:84%
So whatever my
true valuation is,

00:10:10.642 --> 00:10:12.850 align:middle line:84%
I look, and I say, this is
how much I really value A,

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:14.930 align:middle line:84%
this is how much
I really value B,

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:16.890 align:middle line:84%
and this is how much I
really value the bundle.

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:20.530 align:middle line:84%
It's optimal for me to tell the
designer exactly how I really

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:21.910 align:middle line:90%
feel and be honest about it.

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:23.930 align:middle line:84%
And I can't benefit by
misreporting my valuation

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.850 align:middle line:90%
to the designer.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:29.530 align:middle line:84%
All right, any
questions on that?

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:30.290 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:31.957 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Will you
ever have a situation

00:10:31.957 --> 00:10:33.850 align:middle line:84%
where your
externalities are going

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:38.410 align:middle line:84%
to be less than the valuation
that you have for the good?

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.030 align:middle line:84%
That payment would be
negative in essence?

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:42.090 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Good question.

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:43.840 align:middle line:84%
So you're never going
to have a case where

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:45.560 align:middle line:90%
the payments are negative.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:51.560 align:middle line:84%
And this follows from the way
that we define an externality.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:57.500 align:middle line:84%
So, yeah, we have to be a
little careful about this.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:00.065 align:middle line:84%
So it is true that, more
broadly, in economics,

00:11:00.065 --> 00:11:01.440 align:middle line:84%
when we talk about
externalities,

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:03.232 align:middle line:84%
some things do have
positive externalities.

00:11:03.232 --> 00:11:05.388 align:middle line:84%
There are things I can
do that make activities

00:11:05.388 --> 00:11:07.180 align:middle line:84%
I can do that make
other people better off.

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:08.722 align:middle line:84%
So I guess I should
be maybe a little

00:11:08.722 --> 00:11:10.740 align:middle line:84%
careful about the
terminology of externality.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:15.440 align:middle line:84%
So here, what we mean in this
context by externality is,

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.320 align:middle line:84%
well, we're comparing how
well the other bidder would

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:21.720 align:middle line:84%
do if we just cared about
maximizing that bidder's

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.160 align:middle line:84%
utility to how well
that bidder actually

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:25.880 align:middle line:90%
does under this allocation.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:28.720 align:middle line:84%
And there's no way that
the current allocation can

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:30.680 align:middle line:84%
give more to the other
bidder than if we just

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.412 align:middle line:90%
maximize that bidder's utility.

00:11:32.412 --> 00:11:34.120 align:middle line:84%
So for that reason,
this number is always

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.180 align:middle line:90%
going to be nonnegative.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.680 align:middle line:84%
And you're right,
if you're thinking

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:39.180 align:middle line:84%
of other externality context,
this is a bit more special.

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.470 align:middle line:84%
So it does turn out here
that the payments will always

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:42.130 align:middle line:90%
be nonnegative.

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:44.350 align:middle line:84%
They could be 0, but they
would never be negative

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:45.570 align:middle line:84%
because, in order for
them to be negative,

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:47.870 align:middle line:84%
it would have to be that the
allocation we're choosing

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.550 align:middle line:84%
makes the other bidder strictly
better off than the best

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:51.770 align:middle line:90%
allocation for that bidder.

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:54.570 align:middle line:84%
And that goes against
the definition of best.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:55.070 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:58.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: 7 is
maximizing their utility

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:01.550 align:middle line:84%
when everyone besides
myself is in the game?

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:03.930 align:middle line:84%
Or is it just their
utility alone?

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:04.930 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Good question.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:08.550 align:middle line:84%
So the 7 here is--
what we're maximizing

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:14.190 align:middle line:84%
is the sum of everyone
else's utility,

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:16.130 align:middle line:84%
and we're ignoring
my own utility.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:18.693 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, because
there's only two bidders,

00:12:18.693 --> 00:12:20.110 align:middle line:84%
the sum of everyone
else's utility

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:22.410 align:middle line:84%
is just maximizing the
other bidder's utility.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:24.213 align:middle line:84%
But if we had three
bidders, then we

00:12:24.213 --> 00:12:26.630 align:middle line:84%
would think about how could
we allocate the goods in a way

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:30.550 align:middle line:84%
to maximize the sum of, say,
bidders 2 and 3's utility,

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:33.110 align:middle line:84%
ignoring the effect of the
presence of bidder one.

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:34.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, great.

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:36.210 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions on this?

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:39.660 align:middle line:90%


00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:44.540 align:middle line:84%
So now let's change
tacks a little bit.

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:46.620 align:middle line:84%
And we're now going to
move to the final phase

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:50.640 align:middle line:84%
of the course, which is
called dynamic Bayesian games.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:56.780 align:middle line:90%


00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:59.020 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's good at
this point to pause and see

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:00.480 align:middle line:90%
where we've come.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:03.260 align:middle line:84%
So we can think of the course
as organized into four parts.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:07.520 align:middle line:84%
We have looked at static
games and dynamic games.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:11.340 align:middle line:90%


00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:17.100 align:middle line:84%
And we've also looked at
games of complete information

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:18.880 align:middle line:84%
and games of
incomplete information.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:21.500 align:middle line:90%


00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:24.000 align:middle line:84%
And basically, so far, we've
filled in three of these boxes,

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.917 align:middle line:84%
and now we're going to
fill in the fourth box.

00:13:25.917 --> 00:13:27.758 align:middle line:84%
And for each box,
each class of games,

00:13:27.758 --> 00:13:30.300 align:middle line:84%
we have a different solution
concept, a different equilibrium

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:30.960 align:middle line:90%
notion.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:32.040 align:middle line:90%
So let's fill this in.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:37.340 align:middle line:90%


00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:40.120 align:middle line:84%
So in the first part of the
course, we started here.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:43.420 align:middle line:84%
This would just be your
basic strategic-form game

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:44.740 align:middle line:90%
with complete information.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:47.420 align:middle line:84%
And our solution concept there
was just Nash equilibrium.

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:55.580 align:middle line:90%


00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:57.380 align:middle line:90%
Next, we said, well--

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:59.460 align:middle line:84%
we looked at
extensive-form games

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:01.580 align:middle line:84%
where players make
moves over time.

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:04.260 align:middle line:84%
And we said, one problem
of the Nash equilibrium

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:07.580 align:middle line:84%
is it allows players to
make non-credible threats.

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:10.140 align:middle line:84%
And we said we wanted to
refine Nash equilibrium

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:13.580 align:middle line:84%
and impose a stronger solution
concept that rules out

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:15.280 align:middle line:90%
threats that are not credible.

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:17.980 align:middle line:84%
And that solution concept was
called subgame-perfect Nash

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:19.180 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:28.780 align:middle line:90%


00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:31.820 align:middle line:84%
So that was a stronger
notion than Nash

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:33.980 align:middle line:84%
that ruled out some
Nash equilibria

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:36.570 align:middle line:84%
that we didn't find compelling
because they involved threats

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:38.490 align:middle line:84%
that were not
credible at nodes that

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:41.330 align:middle line:90%
were not reached in the game.

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:43.645 align:middle line:84%
Then we stepped back, and
we said, wait a second,

00:14:43.645 --> 00:14:46.270 align:middle line:84%
our complete information seems
like a really strong assumption.

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:48.690 align:middle line:84%
A lot of times when people
come to the game and they start

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:50.950 align:middle line:84%
the game, they know things
that other players don't.

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:52.690 align:middle line:84%
They know things about their
preferences that other players

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:53.650 align:middle line:90%
in the game don't.

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:57.110 align:middle line:84%
So we introduced
Bayesian games, that is,

00:14:57.110 --> 00:14:58.735 align:middle line:84%
games that have
incomplete information,

00:14:58.735 --> 00:15:00.193 align:middle line:84%
and we introduce
a solution concept

00:15:00.193 --> 00:15:02.570 align:middle line:84%
for that called Bayes-Nash
equilibrium or Bayesian Nash

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:03.350 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:15.610 align:middle line:90%


00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:19.190 align:middle line:84%
Now this dealt with the issue
of incomplete information.

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:22.210 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out that
Bayesian Nash equilibrium

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:25.745 align:middle line:84%
doesn't deal with the issue
of non-credible threats.

00:15:25.745 --> 00:15:27.370 align:middle line:84%
And if we want to
look at dynamic games

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:28.913 align:middle line:84%
with incomplete
information, we have

00:15:28.913 --> 00:15:30.330 align:middle line:84%
to both take into
account the fact

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:32.110 align:middle line:84%
that players have
incomplete information.

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:34.800 align:middle line:84%
But we also may want to
rule out equilibria that

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:36.100 align:middle line:90%
involve non-credible threats.

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:37.475 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
introduce today

00:15:37.475 --> 00:15:40.380 align:middle line:84%
our final solution concept,
which it's not very creative.

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:42.325 align:middle line:84%
We basically combine
the perfect from here

00:15:42.325 --> 00:15:44.200 align:middle line:84%
and the Bayes from here,
and it's just called

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:45.660 align:middle line:90%
perfect Bayesian equilibrium.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:16:01.600 align:middle line:90%


00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:04.800 align:middle line:84%
I guess maybe I'll give somewhat
of a Warning about this.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:07.640 align:middle line:84%
These first three
concepts are very--

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:09.420 align:middle line:90%
are well-agreed upon.

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:11.360 align:middle line:84%
Everyone agrees what's
the right notion.

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:14.280 align:middle line:84%
Once we get to dynamic games
of incomplete information,

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:16.040 align:middle line:84%
there's been a lot of
recent work arguing

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:17.780 align:middle line:84%
about what's the right
notion of equilibrium.

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:19.440 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to present
a particular notion called

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:20.893 align:middle line:90%
perfect Bayesian equilibrium.

00:16:20.893 --> 00:16:22.560 align:middle line:84%
But there's 10 other
equilibrium notions

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:24.855 align:middle line:84%
that could fill this
box that are more exotic

00:16:24.855 --> 00:16:26.480 align:middle line:84%
and involve more
constraints and people

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:28.563 align:middle line:84%
are arguing to this day
about what's the right way

00:16:28.563 --> 00:16:29.740 align:middle line:90%
to define equilibrium.

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:31.115 align:middle line:84%
So I'll just warn
you that things

00:16:31.115 --> 00:16:35.060 align:middle line:84%
are a little maybe trickier or
less clear cut in this case,

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:37.600 align:middle line:84%
but we'll try to focus
on well-trodden ground

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:39.000 align:middle line:90%
in this class.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:41.400 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I want to first,
today, before defining

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:43.048 align:middle line:84%
perfect Bayesian
equilibrium, I want

00:16:43.048 --> 00:16:44.840 align:middle line:84%
to give a few examples
that try to motivate

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:48.320 align:middle line:84%
why we need a new definition,
why our existing equilibrium

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:49.650 align:middle line:90%
notions are insufficient.

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:51.400 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll go over
how we can actually

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:52.983 align:middle line:84%
solve for perfect
Bayesian equilibrium

00:16:52.983 --> 00:16:55.320 align:middle line:84%
and go over the
formal definition.

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:59.200 align:middle line:84%
So let's start with
maybe a reminder

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:04.119 align:middle line:84%
about non-credible threats and
a weakness of Nash equilibrium

00:17:04.119 --> 00:17:06.560 align:middle line:84%
with complete information,
and then we'll build on that.

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:09.720 align:middle line:84%
So let's go back to
maybe our classic example

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.800 align:middle line:90%
where we had two firms.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:15.200 align:middle line:84%
We had firm 1,
and we had firm 2.

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:20.960 align:middle line:84%
And we'll think of firm
1 as a potential entrant.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:23.099 align:middle line:84%
And we'll think of
firm 2 as an incumbent.

00:17:23.099 --> 00:17:27.760 align:middle line:90%


00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:29.430 align:middle line:84%
So firm 2's already
in the market.

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:32.330 align:middle line:84%
Firm 1 is deciding whether
to enter the market or not.

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:33.630 align:middle line:84%
So they're called an
entrant, but really, they're

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:34.463 align:middle line:90%
a potential entrant.

00:17:34.463 --> 00:17:35.830 align:middle line:90%
They may or may not enter.

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:39.130 align:middle line:84%
And firm 1 is choosing
whether to enter the market--

00:17:39.130 --> 00:17:40.350 align:middle line:90%
we'll call that E--

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.030 align:middle line:90%
or not enter the market.

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:45.390 align:middle line:84%
We'll call that N. And this is
an example we've done before.

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:46.990 align:middle line:90%
Firm 2 is the incumbent.

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:49.750 align:middle line:84%
If firm 1 enters, firm
2 is the incumbent,

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:52.030 align:middle line:84%
can choose whether to
accommodate their entry

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:53.910 align:middle line:84%
or to fight them by
starting some price war

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:55.702 align:middle line:84%
or trying to cut prices
and doing something

00:17:55.702 --> 00:17:56.950 align:middle line:90%
that hurts both players.

00:17:56.950 --> 00:18:02.270 align:middle line:84%
So firm 2 is choosing A or F, a
for accommodate the new entrant

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:04.870 align:middle line:84%
and f for fight against
the new entrant.

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:07.790 align:middle line:84%
So let's write this as
an extensive-form game.

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:09.210 align:middle line:90%
We have firm 1 is here.

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:12.190 align:middle line:90%


00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:14.830 align:middle line:84%
They're choosing whether to--
maybe I'll put N here, not

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:16.790 align:middle line:90%
enter or enter.

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:19.990 align:middle line:84%
And then if they do
enter, we have firm 2.

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:23.030 align:middle line:84%
And they're choosing to
either accommodate the entrant

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:24.090 align:middle line:90%
or fight.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:27.860 align:middle line:84%
Let me make sure I
get the numbers right.

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:37.940 align:middle line:84%
So what we did here, let's put
0, 5, 1, 1, and negative 10, 10

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:39.180 align:middle line:90%
to make this easy.

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:41.220 align:middle line:90%
So what's the idea?

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:43.687 align:middle line:84%
If firm 1 does not
enter, well, that's

00:18:43.687 --> 00:18:45.020 align:middle line:90%
great for firm 2, the incumbent.

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:45.740 align:middle line:90%
They're the monopolist.

00:18:45.740 --> 00:18:46.823 align:middle line:90%
They're all by themselves.

00:18:46.823 --> 00:18:48.220 align:middle line:90%
So they get a payoff of 5.

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:51.380 align:middle line:84%
Firm 1 isn't entering, so
they get a payoff of 0.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:54.140 align:middle line:84%
If firm 1 does enter, well,
then the payoffs of the firms

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:57.700 align:middle line:84%
depend on whether to accommodate
the entrant or fights.

00:18:57.700 --> 00:19:00.080 align:middle line:84%
If they accommodate, then
they both do pretty well.

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:02.900 align:middle line:84%
They both get 1, but of
course, not as well as firm 2

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:06.565 align:middle line:84%
would've gotten if firm 2
were by himself as monopolist.

00:19:06.565 --> 00:19:07.940 align:middle line:84%
And then if firm
2 fights, that's

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:09.680 align:middle line:90%
really bad for both sides.

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:10.560 align:middle line:90%
They a price war.

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:12.080 align:middle line:90%
Prices go, say, really low.

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:14.100 align:middle line:90%
And this is bad for everyone.

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:16.560 align:middle line:84%
We're thinking of fighting
in an abstract sense.

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:18.060 align:middle line:84%
It could literally
involve violence,

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:19.340 align:middle line:84%
or it could be
cutting prices, or it

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:20.320 align:middle line:90%
could be flooding the market.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:21.900 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of
things it could be.

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:26.650 align:middle line:84%
So let's now just write
this as a bit of a review

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:30.750 align:middle line:84%
today of our standard
strategic form game box.

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:34.330 align:middle line:84%
Player 1 has two
strategies, N, E. Player 2

00:19:34.330 --> 00:19:41.210 align:middle line:84%
has two strategies, A, F. N, E
to keep the order consistent.

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:42.250 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:19:42.250 --> 00:19:46.190 align:middle line:84%
If player 1 doesn't enter, then
they both get 0, 5 either way.

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:49.930 align:middle line:90%


00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:52.290 align:middle line:84%
If player 1 does
enter, then the payoffs

00:19:52.290 --> 00:19:53.550 align:middle line:90%
depend on what player 2 does.

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:58.590 align:middle line:84%
And we get 1, 1 here and
negative 10, negative 10 here.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:01.655 align:middle line:90%


00:20:01.655 --> 00:20:03.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, now let's just do
our standard approach

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:05.582 align:middle line:84%
to try to find the Nash
equilibria of this.

00:20:05.582 --> 00:20:06.790 align:middle line:90%
This is a good little review.

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:08.498 align:middle line:84%
The final exam is
going to be cumulative.

00:20:08.498 --> 00:20:11.988 align:middle line:84%
So you do have to remember
how to solve the basic games.

00:20:11.988 --> 00:20:12.530 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:13.510 align:middle line:90%
We go down here.

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:16.250 align:middle line:84%
If firm 2 is going
to accommodate,

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:18.730 align:middle line:84%
then it's better
for firm 1 to enter.

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:21.970 align:middle line:84%
If firm 2 is going to fight,
it's better for firm 1

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:23.210 align:middle line:90%
to not enter.

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:25.370 align:middle line:84%
And then we can do
things the other way.

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:27.110 align:middle line:84%
If firm 1 doesn't
enter-- actually,

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:28.890 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't matter
what firm 2 does.

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:32.330 align:middle line:84%
If firm 1 enters, then firm
2 would rather accommodate.

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:34.650 align:middle line:90%
So we get two equilibria.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:46.350 align:middle line:84%
We get EA and NF, which we
can see on our graph here.

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:51.210 align:middle line:90%


00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:54.370 align:middle line:84%
Now, both of these
are Nash equilibria.

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:57.970 align:middle line:84%
But we precisely introduced
subgame-perfect Nash equilibria

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:00.050 align:middle line:84%
as a more demanding
solution concept

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:02.730 align:middle line:84%
to rule out one of
these equilibria.

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:06.450 align:middle line:84%
So which of these equilibria
is not a subgame-perfect Nash

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:07.070 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium?

00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:08.170 align:middle line:90%
Which one did we--

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:10.470 align:middle line:84%
does subgame
perfection rule out?

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:15.990 align:middle line:90%


00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:16.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:19.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
because you can't really--

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:23.960 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah, sorry, NF or EA.

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:25.140 align:middle line:90%
It's one of these two.

00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:26.375 align:middle line:90%
It's EA and NF.

00:21:26.375 --> 00:21:27.000 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:30.280 align:middle line:90%


00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:31.380 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Maybe, yeah.

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:32.440 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: NF.

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:33.720 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: NF, I agree, OK.

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:35.400 align:middle line:90%
And why?

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:38.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because if
the entrant enters,

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:44.000 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't make sense for
the incumbent to fight.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:45.000 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:48.160 align:middle line:84%
So both of these
are Nash equilibria.

00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:51.020 align:middle line:84%
Only EA is a subgame-perfect
Nash equilibrium.

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:53.380 align:middle line:84%
As you said, NF is
not subgame-perfect.

00:21:53.380 --> 00:22:00.520 align:middle line:90%


00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:03.160 align:middle line:84%
And it's not subgame-perfect
because it involves a threat

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:06.200 align:middle line:90%
by firm 2 that is not credible.

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:11.120 align:middle line:84%
Firm 2 says, if you
enter, I'm going to fight.

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:13.522 align:middle line:84%
But if firm 1 actually
enters, well, firm 2 is here,

00:22:13.522 --> 00:22:14.980 align:middle line:84%
and they look,
well, wait a second.

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:17.040 align:middle line:90%
If I fight, I get negative 10.

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:18.740 align:middle line:90%
If I accommodate, I get 1.

00:22:18.740 --> 00:22:20.870 align:middle line:84%
I'm much better
off accommodating.

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:24.830 align:middle line:84%
So it's not optimal for
firm 2 to actually carry out

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:26.990 align:middle line:84%
this punishment or
this threat that they

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:29.070 align:middle line:90%
threatened to carry out.

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:33.190 align:middle line:84%
Formally, well, we have
to think of the definition

00:22:33.190 --> 00:22:36.050 align:middle line:84%
of subgame-perfect
Nash equilibrium.

00:22:36.050 --> 00:22:38.390 align:middle line:84%
In the definition, we have
to look at every subgame.

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:40.470 align:middle line:84%
We have the full game
here, but we also

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:42.070 align:middle line:90%
have subgame that starts here.

00:22:42.070 --> 00:22:47.350 align:middle line:84%
So remember, this is one
subgame of the full game.

00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:51.070 align:middle line:84%
And the problem with NF
is that fighting is not

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:52.750 align:middle line:90%
an equilibrium of this subgame.

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:54.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, it may be weird to
think of this as subgame

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.375 align:middle line:84%
because there's only
one player moving.

00:22:56.375 --> 00:22:59.110 align:middle line:84%
But remember, we can have a
game with a single player,

00:22:59.110 --> 00:23:01.190 align:middle line:84%
and an equilibrium of
a single-player game

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:04.030 align:middle line:84%
just means that player's
behaving optimally.

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:08.690 align:middle line:84%
So here F is not a Nash
equilibrium of this subgame

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:10.990 align:middle line:84%
because F is strictly
worse for player 2

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:13.470 align:middle line:84%
than A. So this doesn't
satisfy our definition

00:23:13.470 --> 00:23:18.260 align:middle line:84%
of subgame-perfect
Nash equilibrium.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:21.840 align:middle line:84%
But let's maybe change
the game slightly.

00:23:21.840 --> 00:23:25.530 align:middle line:90%


00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:27.780 align:middle line:84%
So what if we change the
game and say, well, actually,

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:32.920 align:middle line:84%
firm 1 has two different
ways of entering.

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:35.400 align:middle line:84%
They can enter the market
and produce one kind of good,

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:37.260 align:middle line:84%
or they can enter the market
and produce a slightly different

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:38.080 align:middle line:90%
kind of good.

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:39.747 align:middle line:84%
So we're now going
to say that firm 1 is

00:23:39.747 --> 00:23:45.445 align:middle line:84%
choosing between not entering
or I'll call it E1 or E2.

00:23:45.445 --> 00:23:46.820 align:middle line:84%
These are just
two different ways

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:48.780 align:middle line:84%
they can enter and maybe use
a slightly different marketing

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:50.960 align:middle line:84%
strategy or slightly
different pricing strategy,

00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:54.620 align:middle line:84%
but they're effectively
entering either way.

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:58.620 align:middle line:84%
And now let's write
out how this looks.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:01.780 align:middle line:90%
So we have firm 1 here.

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:09.700 align:middle line:84%
Firm 1 is now choosing enter,
not enter-- sorry, not enter,

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:12.740 align:middle line:90%
E1 or E2.

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:16.060 align:middle line:84%
Again, if they don't
enter, the payoff is 0, 5.

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:18.860 align:middle line:84%
If they enter in
this way, firm 2

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:21.060 align:middle line:90%
chooses to accommodate a fight.

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:22.180 align:middle line:90%
They enter in this way.

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:24.300 align:middle line:84%
Firm 2 chooses to
commentator or fight.

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:27.220 align:middle line:90%
This is still firm 2, firm 2.

00:24:27.220 --> 00:24:30.580 align:middle line:84%
And maybe the exact
pricing strategy

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:32.200 align:middle line:84%
they use makes a
bit of a difference.

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:33.783 align:middle line:84%
But just to make
things simpler, let's

00:24:33.783 --> 00:24:36.583 align:middle line:84%
say it doesn't really affect
their payoffs whether they sell

00:24:36.583 --> 00:24:38.000 align:middle line:84%
them in one way
or a different way

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:39.460 align:middle line:84%
or whether they use
this advertising firm

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:40.837 align:middle line:90%
or this other advertising firm.

00:24:40.837 --> 00:24:42.420 align:middle line:84%
So let's say the
payoffs down here are

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:46.660 align:middle line:84%
going to be 1, 1, negative
10, negative 10, 1, 1,

00:24:46.660 --> 00:24:49.100 align:middle line:90%
negative 10, negative 10.

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:51.000 align:middle line:84%
So these are different
ways of entering.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:54.155 align:middle line:84%
But the effect on the
payoffs is actually 0.

00:24:54.155 --> 00:24:56.280 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't actually change
the way the payoffs are.

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:58.280 align:middle line:84%
And we could change these
slightly if we wanted,

00:24:58.280 --> 00:25:00.500 align:middle line:90%
but it doesn't matter.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:05.180 align:middle line:84%
And notice, the way
I'm writing this,

00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:08.420 align:middle line:84%
just let me slightly
modify this.

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:10.140 align:middle line:84%
We have to make a
choice, does firm 2

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:14.080 align:middle line:84%
observe the advertising
strategy that firm 1 is using?

00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:15.370 align:middle line:90%
And we're going to assume no.

00:25:15.370 --> 00:25:18.470 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to put this
in the same information set.

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:20.793 align:middle line:84%
So as firm 2, all I see is
that firm 1 has entered.

00:25:20.793 --> 00:25:22.210 align:middle line:84%
I don't really
know under the Hood

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:24.530 align:middle line:84%
exactly what, say, production
process they're using

00:25:24.530 --> 00:25:26.550 align:middle line:84%
or what advertising
firm they are.

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:30.785 align:middle line:90%


00:25:30.785 --> 00:25:36.410 align:middle line:84%
And now let's think of the
strategies in this game.

00:25:36.410 --> 00:25:38.810 align:middle line:84%
It turns out the
strategies in this game

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:41.730 align:middle line:84%
are exactly the same as
the strategies over here.

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:44.050 align:middle line:90%
Firm 1 is still choosing--

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:47.250 align:middle line:84%
sorry, for firm 1, it's
slightly different.

00:25:47.250 --> 00:25:52.890 align:middle line:84%
So firm 1 is choosing
between N, E1, and E2.

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:57.833 align:middle line:84%
And firm 2 is still choosing
accommodate or fight.

00:25:57.833 --> 00:25:59.250 align:middle line:84%
It's the same for
firm 2, but it's

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:02.622 align:middle line:90%
slightly different from firm 1.

00:26:02.622 --> 00:26:04.330 align:middle line:84%
Let's try to compute
the Nash-- let's try

00:26:04.330 --> 00:26:15.400 align:middle line:84%
to find the Nash
equilibria of this game.

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:21.900 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is what are some
Nash equilibria of this game?

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:22.900 align:middle line:90%
It looks really similar.

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:27.920 align:middle line:84%
So I think it shouldn't be
too hard to figure this out.

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:31.160 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's organize it
by what firm 2's doing.

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:33.200 align:middle line:84%
Let's say firm 2
is accommodating.

00:26:33.200 --> 00:26:36.020 align:middle line:84%
And notice firm 2 makes a single
choice-- accommodate or fight.

00:26:36.020 --> 00:26:38.120 align:middle line:84%
It's a single choice
because these two nodes are

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:39.540 align:middle line:90%
in the same information set.

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:41.960 align:middle line:84%
So I can't choose to
accommodate E1 and fight

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:44.360 align:middle line:84%
against E2 because I
can't distinguish those.

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:47.920 align:middle line:84%
So if firm 2 accommodates,
what does firm 1 want to do?

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:48.520 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:26:48.520 --> 00:26:49.500 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: E1 or E2.

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:51.300 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: E1 and E2 is
going to be good for them.

00:26:51.300 --> 00:26:53.592 align:middle line:84%
They want to enter, and
they're happy to do either one.

00:26:53.592 --> 00:26:58.240 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to get
E1A and E2A are both

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:01.500 align:middle line:90%
going to be Nash equilibrium.

00:27:01.500 --> 00:27:04.040 align:middle line:90%
What if firm 2's going to fight?

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:05.380 align:middle line:90%
What does firm 1 want to do?

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:09.156 align:middle line:90%


00:27:09.156 --> 00:27:09.656 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:27:09.656 --> 00:27:10.600 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Not enter.

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:11.433 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Not enter.

00:27:11.433 --> 00:27:15.160 align:middle line:84%
So now it looks
similar to before.

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:17.320 align:middle line:84%
But now we actually
have three equilibria.

00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:20.120 align:middle line:90%


00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:23.260 align:middle line:84%
Same idea, if firm 1 is
going to accommodate, then--

00:27:23.260 --> 00:27:23.760 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:25.640 align:middle line:84%
If firm 2 is going
to accommodate,

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:27.260 align:middle line:90%
then firm 1 wants to enter.

00:27:27.260 --> 00:27:28.880 align:middle line:84%
They're happy to
enter either way.

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:33.960 align:middle line:84%
If firm 2's going to fight, then
firm 1 doesn't want to enter.

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:39.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, I think that NF still feels
like a non-credible threat.

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:43.680 align:middle line:90%


00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:46.160 align:middle line:84%
For exactly the same
reason as before.

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:48.265 align:middle line:90%
Firm 2 is fighting.

00:27:48.265 --> 00:27:49.640 align:middle line:84%
They know that,
by fighting, they

00:27:49.640 --> 00:27:52.340 align:middle line:84%
get a payoff of negative 10,
and if they didn't fight,

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:53.840 align:middle line:90%
they would get a payoff of 1.

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:56.040 align:middle line:84%
So to me, this feels like
a non-credible threat.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:59.680 align:middle line:84%
But the question is, is NF a
subgame-perfect Nash equilibrium

00:27:59.680 --> 00:28:00.380 align:middle line:90%
of this game?

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:03.310 align:middle line:90%


00:28:03.310 --> 00:28:04.623 align:middle line:90%
What do people think?

00:28:04.623 --> 00:28:06.040 align:middle line:84%
Well, the first
question we always

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:08.417 align:middle line:84%
have to ask ourselves
about subgame-perfect Nash

00:28:08.417 --> 00:28:10.250 align:middle line:84%
is, first, we have to
identify the subgames.

00:28:10.250 --> 00:28:12.167 align:middle line:84%
Remember, maybe we're
going a little bit back.

00:28:12.167 --> 00:28:13.550 align:middle line:90%
We need to review a little bit.

00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:16.150 align:middle line:84%
So if we go all
the way back, what

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:20.350 align:middle line:84%
are the subgames
of this game here?

00:28:20.350 --> 00:28:22.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Only if the
entire game is a subgame

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:25.670 align:middle line:84%
because the decision
point for player 2

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:27.290 align:middle line:90%
is not a singleton node.

00:28:27.290 --> 00:28:28.290 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:32.070 align:middle line:84%
So we can't start a subgame
here because we would

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:33.790 align:middle line:90%
break this information set.

00:28:33.790 --> 00:28:36.310 align:middle line:84%
We can't start here because we
would break this information

00:28:36.310 --> 00:28:36.850 align:middle line:90%
set.

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:40.350 align:middle line:84%
And we can't include both of
them as part of our subgame

00:28:40.350 --> 00:28:42.430 align:middle line:84%
because we had a rule
that a subgame has

00:28:42.430 --> 00:28:44.510 align:middle line:90%
to start with a single node.

00:28:44.510 --> 00:28:47.070 align:middle line:84%
So the only subgame
of the full game

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:49.430 align:middle line:90%
is just the full game itself.

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:53.170 align:middle line:84%
And if there's no
proper subgames,

00:28:53.170 --> 00:28:55.310 align:middle line:84%
no subgames other
than the game itself,

00:28:55.310 --> 00:28:57.270 align:middle line:84%
then subgame-perfect
Nash equilibrium

00:28:57.270 --> 00:29:00.990 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have any additional bite
because the additional strength

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:04.067 align:middle line:84%
of subgame-perfect Nash is
it says, in every subgame,

00:29:04.067 --> 00:29:05.650 align:middle line:84%
we must be playing
a Nash equilibrium.

00:29:05.650 --> 00:29:06.850 align:middle line:84%
But if there's no
other subgames,

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:08.260 align:middle line:90%
well, it doesn't have any bite.

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:12.586 align:middle line:84%
So Nash equilibria
and all of these--

00:29:12.586 --> 00:29:16.440 align:middle line:90%
all three are subgame-perfect.

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:25.980 align:middle line:90%


00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:28.000 align:middle line:84%
So this seems like
maybe a problem.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:31.900 align:middle line:84%
It shows that our notion
of subgame perfection

00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:35.740 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have the bite to
rule out non-credible threats

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:37.860 align:middle line:84%
in games like this where
we have multiple nodes

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:39.235 align:middle line:90%
in the same information set.

00:29:39.235 --> 00:29:40.860 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
introduce our notion

00:29:40.860 --> 00:29:43.140 align:middle line:84%
of perfect Bayesian
equilibrium to rule out

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:44.800 align:middle line:84%
these kinds of
non-credible threats,

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:47.380 align:middle line:90%
even in situations like this.

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:50.680 align:middle line:84%
So let's maybe go through
a more involved example.

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:55.140 align:middle line:90%


00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:58.700 align:middle line:84%
So let's think of, maybe we'll
call this, an application game.

00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:04.540 align:middle line:84%
People have probably applied to
internships or jobs or schools

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:05.360 align:middle line:90%
like this.

00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:07.632 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, there's
an optional essay

00:30:07.632 --> 00:30:09.590 align:middle line:84%
or an optional interview
or something optional.

00:30:09.590 --> 00:30:11.190 align:middle line:84%
And often, people feel
it's not really optional.

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:12.510 align:middle line:84%
If I don't do it, I'm
not going to get the job.

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:13.390 align:middle line:90%
I'm not going to get in.

00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:15.265 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's try to
explore that in this game.

00:30:15.265 --> 00:30:18.690 align:middle line:84%
So let's say, well, it's
an application game.

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:19.910 align:middle line:90%
We have an applicant.

00:30:19.910 --> 00:30:23.370 align:middle line:90%


00:30:23.370 --> 00:30:26.170 align:middle line:84%
And we could call it a firm
or a college or a school.

00:30:26.170 --> 00:30:26.950 align:middle line:90%
Let's say school.

00:30:26.950 --> 00:30:30.690 align:middle line:84%
Let's use school just in this
example, so we have a school.

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:35.730 align:middle line:84%
And the applicant, let's
assume, has private information.

00:30:35.730 --> 00:30:38.458 align:middle line:84%
In this example, it could be
private information about how

00:30:38.458 --> 00:30:39.750 align:middle line:90%
strong of a candidate they are.

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:42.350 align:middle line:84%
But maybe, here, just to
make it a little nicer,

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:45.130 align:middle line:84%
we'll say the applicant
privately knows how excited they

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:46.770 align:middle line:90%
are about going to this school.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:50.190 align:middle line:90%
So the applicant has two types.

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:51.450 align:middle line:90%
We'll call them types.

00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:59.850 align:middle line:84%
Maybe t excited or passionate,
let's say P, tP or tN.

00:30:59.850 --> 00:31:01.802 align:middle line:84%
So tP says, I'm really
passionate about going

00:31:01.802 --> 00:31:04.010 align:middle line:84%
to this school about the
thing that this school does.

00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:05.490 align:middle line:90%
I'm I want to go.

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:08.270 align:middle line:84%
And tN doesn't really--
they're not quite as excited.

00:31:08.270 --> 00:31:12.610 align:middle line:84%
They still want to go, but
they're not quite as excited.

00:31:12.610 --> 00:31:16.073 align:middle line:84%
And then we have the
school and the school--

00:31:16.073 --> 00:31:17.490 align:middle line:84%
let's just say in
words, what they

00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:28.855 align:middle line:84%
do is they want to
accept tP but not tN.

00:31:28.855 --> 00:31:30.230 align:middle line:84%
So there's two
kinds of students,

00:31:30.230 --> 00:31:31.470 align:middle line:90%
two kinds of applicants.

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:33.370 align:middle line:84%
I'll be more precise about what
the utilities are, but just

00:31:33.370 --> 00:31:35.198 align:middle line:84%
at a high level to
understand, if someone's

00:31:35.198 --> 00:31:36.990 align:middle line:84%
really passionate and
excited about coming,

00:31:36.990 --> 00:31:38.630 align:middle line:90%
they want to accept that person.

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:40.922 align:middle line:84%
If they're not excited, they
don't want to accept them.

00:31:40.922 --> 00:31:42.210 align:middle line:90%
So let's try to--

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:45.890 align:middle line:90%
let's draw out a game tree here.

00:31:45.890 --> 00:31:47.970 align:middle line:84%
So the applicant has
private information

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:50.170 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning of the
game about whether they're

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:52.090 align:middle line:84%
excited or not about
going to the school.

00:31:52.090 --> 00:31:54.450 align:middle line:84%
And the way that we model
this is we start with a move

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:55.330 align:middle line:90%
by nature.

00:31:55.330 --> 00:31:57.450 align:middle line:84%
We first assume that
nature makes a move

00:31:57.450 --> 00:31:59.930 align:middle line:90%
and draws this applicant's type.

00:31:59.930 --> 00:32:02.030 align:middle line:84%
And we need to specify
some probabilities.

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:06.980 align:middle line:84%
So let's say that they're
passionate with probability 1/2,

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:08.980 align:middle line:84%
and they're not passionate
with probability 1/2,

00:32:08.980 --> 00:32:12.570 align:middle line:84%
just to make the math
a little bit easier.

00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:15.320 align:middle line:84%
So I need a new
board just to-- this

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:17.960 align:middle line:84%
is going to get a
little big, this game.

00:32:17.960 --> 00:32:18.920 align:middle line:90%
Let's start here.

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:23.000 align:middle line:90%
So we start with nature.

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:25.920 align:middle line:84%
And nature is first going to
draw whether this person is

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:27.480 align:middle line:90%
passionate or not.

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:30.720 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to have nature
making this first move,

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:33.540 align:middle line:84%
probability 1/2,
and probability 1/2.

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:37.900 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to be
the passionate student.

00:32:37.900 --> 00:32:40.682 align:middle line:90%


00:32:40.682 --> 00:32:42.640 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be the
non-passionate student.

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:45.160 align:middle line:90%


00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:49.480 align:middle line:84%
And then the student
or the applicant--

00:32:49.480 --> 00:32:52.040 align:middle line:84%
I'm calling them the
applicant, so I'll say A--

00:32:52.040 --> 00:32:53.420 align:middle line:90%
is going to make a choice.

00:32:53.420 --> 00:32:57.220 align:middle line:84%
They can either submit
the optional essay or not.

00:32:57.220 --> 00:32:58.800 align:middle line:90%
They're applying to this job--

00:32:58.800 --> 00:32:59.960 align:middle line:90%
or this school.

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:01.390 align:middle line:90%
There's some optional essay.

00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:12.290 align:middle line:84%
So let's write here, let's say
I can include the essay or not.

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:13.910 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll use these words.

00:33:13.910 --> 00:33:17.350 align:middle line:84%
So they're applying either
way, but they're just choosing

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:18.610 align:middle line:90%
whether to submit the essay.

00:33:18.610 --> 00:33:27.650 align:middle line:90%


00:33:27.650 --> 00:33:37.470 align:middle line:84%
And now we have the
school is making a choice

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:41.030 align:middle line:84%
whether to accept or
reject the candidate.

00:33:41.030 --> 00:33:46.310 align:middle line:84%
Let's say accept,
reject, and let's save

00:33:46.310 --> 00:33:57.750 align:middle line:90%
some space over here, A, R. OK.

00:33:57.750 --> 00:34:00.700 align:middle line:84%
Now, we said the
school would rather

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:04.220 align:middle line:84%
accept someone who's passionate
than someone who's not.

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:11.580 align:middle line:84%
And let's say that a passionate
candidate gives the school

00:34:11.580 --> 00:34:14.820 align:middle line:84%
a utility of 3, but
someone who's not excited

00:34:14.820 --> 00:34:17.540 align:middle line:84%
gives the school a payoff of
negative 2, if they accept them.

00:34:17.540 --> 00:34:20.580 align:middle line:84%
And if they don't accept them,
they just get a payoff of 0.

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:22.699 align:middle line:84%
So let's go and
fill that in here.

00:34:22.699 --> 00:34:24.880 align:middle line:84%
On this side of the tree,
the student is excited.

00:34:24.880 --> 00:34:25.820 align:middle line:90%
They're passionate.

00:34:25.820 --> 00:34:27.860 align:middle line:84%
So if the school
accepts them, the school

00:34:27.860 --> 00:34:31.699 align:middle line:90%
is going to get a payoff of 3.

00:34:31.699 --> 00:34:33.715 align:middle line:84%
And if they reject
them, the school's

00:34:33.715 --> 00:34:34.840 align:middle line:90%
going to get a payoff of 0.

00:34:34.840 --> 00:34:36.423 align:middle line:84%
I'm writing a comma
here because we're

00:34:36.423 --> 00:34:38.320 align:middle line:84%
going to fill in the
applicants payoffs next.

00:34:38.320 --> 00:34:38.820 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:34:38.820 --> 00:34:42.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there a
reason why there are no--

00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:44.020 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:34:44.020 --> 00:34:46.915 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yes, I going to
fill it out, but maybe I'll--

00:34:46.915 --> 00:34:49.040 align:middle line:84%
let me fill out the payoffs,
and then I'll do that.

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:52.540 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to fill in
some information sets next.

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:56.300 align:middle line:84%
And then if we go over here,
if we accept the not very

00:34:56.300 --> 00:35:01.420 align:middle line:84%
excited student, then we
get negative 2, negative 2,

00:35:01.420 --> 00:35:03.340 align:middle line:90%
and then 0, 0.

00:35:03.340 --> 00:35:07.620 align:middle line:84%
But as you pointed
out, the school

00:35:07.620 --> 00:35:09.940 align:middle line:84%
only observes whether
the applicant has

00:35:09.940 --> 00:35:11.163 align:middle line:90%
submitted the optional essay.

00:35:11.163 --> 00:35:13.580 align:middle line:84%
They don't directly observe
whether the student is excited

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:14.500 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:35:14.500 --> 00:35:19.780 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to need to define
our information sets like this.

00:35:19.780 --> 00:35:20.400 align:middle line:90%
Here.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:24.060 align:middle line:90%


00:35:24.060 --> 00:35:25.740 align:middle line:90%
And like this.

00:35:25.740 --> 00:35:27.660 align:middle line:84%
So the school has
two information sets.

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:30.940 align:middle line:84%
At this information set,
I know that you've not

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:36.500 align:middle line:84%
submitted the essay, but I
don't know your true enthusiasm.

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:40.420 align:middle line:84%
At this information set, I know
you've submitted the essay,

00:35:40.420 --> 00:35:44.620 align:middle line:84%
but again, I don't know
your true enthusiasm.

00:35:44.620 --> 00:35:48.940 align:middle line:84%
Now we need to specify
the student's payoff.

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:51.740 align:middle line:84%
And let's start,
when the student

00:35:51.740 --> 00:35:55.180 align:middle line:84%
doesn't submit the
essay, let's suppose

00:35:55.180 --> 00:36:00.570 align:middle line:84%
that if they're rejected,
they get a payoff of 0.

00:36:00.570 --> 00:36:03.810 align:middle line:84%
And actually, let's
assume that everywhere.

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:06.650 align:middle line:84%
So all I've said here is
whenever the student is

00:36:06.650 --> 00:36:08.570 align:middle line:84%
rejected, they get a
payoff of 0 because they

00:36:08.570 --> 00:36:09.830 align:middle line:90%
want to be accepted.

00:36:09.830 --> 00:36:12.970 align:middle line:90%


00:36:12.970 --> 00:36:17.610 align:middle line:84%
Now, if they do get accepted,
let's suppose that--

00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:19.910 align:middle line:84%
so let's talk about the
value of acceptance.

00:36:19.910 --> 00:36:24.130 align:middle line:90%


00:36:24.130 --> 00:36:29.610 align:middle line:84%
Well it's 2 to the
passionate student.

00:36:29.610 --> 00:36:32.170 align:middle line:84%
It's only 1 to the less
passionate student.

00:36:32.170 --> 00:36:34.650 align:middle line:84%
And let's assume that
writing the essay is costly.

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:37.830 align:middle line:84%
So writing the essay
also has a cost of c.

00:36:37.830 --> 00:36:41.530 align:middle line:90%


00:36:41.530 --> 00:36:43.990 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just using c so I
can vary this over the game.

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:45.190 align:middle line:90%
But c is a known parameter.

00:36:45.190 --> 00:36:47.510 align:middle line:84%
We all know c is just like
a number like 1 and 2,

00:36:47.510 --> 00:36:50.830 align:middle line:84%
but I just want to have some
flexibility over what this is.

00:36:50.830 --> 00:36:52.350 align:middle line:84%
So let's see if we
can fill this in.

00:36:52.350 --> 00:36:55.760 align:middle line:84%
So down here, the
student is passionate,

00:36:55.760 --> 00:36:59.160 align:middle line:84%
and they don't submit the essay,
and then they get accepted.

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:00.560 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:02.400 align:middle line:90%
Question.

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:04.065 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess
we'll modify it,

00:37:04.065 --> 00:37:06.440 align:middle line:84%
but for the one where the
student writes the essay and is

00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:08.940 align:middle line:84%
rejected, would
it be [INAUDIBLE]?

00:37:08.940 --> 00:37:11.320 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Good, good,
yes, we should do that.

00:37:11.320 --> 00:37:12.135 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:37:12.135 --> 00:37:13.260 align:middle line:90%
They still had to write it.

00:37:13.260 --> 00:37:15.140 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, great point.

00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:15.980 align:middle line:90%
Let's do that.

00:37:15.980 --> 00:37:20.360 align:middle line:90%


00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:23.000 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:24.720 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:26.380 align:middle line:90%
OK, thanks.

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:27.700 align:middle line:90%
So let's have that in here.

00:37:27.700 --> 00:37:28.700 align:middle line:90%
And now we go over here.

00:37:28.700 --> 00:37:29.242 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:37:29.242 --> 00:37:31.408 align:middle line:90%
The student is passionate.

00:37:31.408 --> 00:37:33.700 align:middle line:84%
They don't write the essay,
and then they get accepted.

00:37:33.700 --> 00:37:37.760 align:middle line:84%
So they get 2 for being
passionate, and that's it.

00:37:37.760 --> 00:37:39.505 align:middle line:84%
Over here, the
student is passionate,

00:37:39.505 --> 00:37:40.880 align:middle line:84%
but they have to
write the essay,

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:42.180 align:middle line:90%
and then they're accepted.

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:45.920 align:middle line:90%
So we get 2 minus c.

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:47.240 align:middle line:90%
Then we go over here.

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:48.818 align:middle line:90%
The student is not passionate.

00:37:48.818 --> 00:37:50.860 align:middle line:84%
They don't write the essay,
and they're accepted.

00:37:50.860 --> 00:37:54.030 align:middle line:90%
So they get 1.

00:37:54.030 --> 00:37:56.370 align:middle line:84%
And then, over here, the
student is not passionate.

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:59.480 align:middle line:84%
And they do write the
essay so they get 1 minus.

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:01.530 align:middle line:84%
It's maybe getting
a little messy,

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:05.370 align:middle line:90%
but I think we have everything.

00:38:05.370 --> 00:38:07.510 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll put parentheses
around these things just

00:38:07.510 --> 00:38:09.010 align:middle line:90%
to make it a little clearer.

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:16.190 align:middle line:90%


00:38:16.190 --> 00:38:22.070 align:middle line:90%
OK, so here's our game.

00:38:22.070 --> 00:38:23.950 align:middle line:90%
Let's go back over here.

00:38:23.950 --> 00:38:25.010 align:middle line:90%
Start analyzing.

00:38:25.010 --> 00:38:44.127 align:middle line:90%


00:38:44.127 --> 00:38:46.710 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to look at a few
different versions of the game

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:48.377 align:middle line:84%
and a few different
kinds of equilibria.

00:38:48.377 --> 00:38:55.030 align:middle line:84%
But let's first assume
that c is less than 1.

00:38:55.030 --> 00:38:57.453 align:middle line:84%
so this means even if
I'm not passionate,

00:38:57.453 --> 00:38:58.870 align:middle line:84%
if I'm going to
get accepted, it's

00:38:58.870 --> 00:39:01.350 align:middle line:90%
still worth writing the essay.

00:39:01.350 --> 00:39:07.790 align:middle line:84%
The benefit of being accepted
outweighs the essay cost.

00:39:07.790 --> 00:39:11.550 align:middle line:84%
And let's see if we can
look for an equilibrium

00:39:11.550 --> 00:39:14.365 align:middle line:84%
where every student
applies and writes

00:39:14.365 --> 00:39:15.990 align:middle line:84%
the essay because I
think this is often

00:39:15.990 --> 00:39:16.970 align:middle line:90%
what we see in practice.

00:39:16.970 --> 00:39:18.450 align:middle line:84%
Even the students who
aren't that excited,

00:39:18.450 --> 00:39:19.630 align:middle line:84%
they write some essay
about how excited

00:39:19.630 --> 00:39:20.838 align:middle line:90%
they are to go to the school.

00:39:20.838 --> 00:39:22.970 align:middle line:84%
So let's look for
an equilibrium.

00:39:22.970 --> 00:39:33.710 align:middle line:90%


00:39:33.710 --> 00:39:43.090 align:middle line:84%
In which both types of
students submit the essay.

00:39:43.090 --> 00:39:48.656 align:middle line:90%


00:39:48.656 --> 00:39:53.820 align:middle line:84%
So formally, let's look
at the student strategy.

00:39:53.820 --> 00:39:56.510 align:middle line:84%
Remember, a student strategy
has to say what the student does

00:39:56.510 --> 00:39:58.260 align:middle line:84%
as a function of their
type, as a function

00:39:58.260 --> 00:40:00.100 align:middle line:90%
of their private information.

00:40:00.100 --> 00:40:03.300 align:middle line:84%
So we have the
passionate student

00:40:03.300 --> 00:40:06.800 align:middle line:84%
and the non-passionate student,
and they both choose the essay.

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:12.780 align:middle line:90%


00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:14.260 align:middle line:90%
It's pretty easy.

00:40:14.260 --> 00:40:21.660 align:middle line:84%
And now we have to go to the
school and see what they do.

00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:26.500 align:middle line:84%
So let's put ourselves in
the position of the school.

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:27.900 align:middle line:84%
And let's look at
the information

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:30.420 align:middle line:84%
set where they have
received an essay.

00:40:30.420 --> 00:40:33.380 align:middle line:84%
So remember, for the
school, their information

00:40:33.380 --> 00:40:36.000 align:middle line:90%
sets are essay and no essay.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:40.740 align:middle line:90%


00:40:40.740 --> 00:40:42.460 align:middle line:84%
So the school needs
to decide what

00:40:42.460 --> 00:40:45.413 align:middle line:84%
to do if they see that the
student has submitted an essay

00:40:45.413 --> 00:40:47.330 align:middle line:84%
and what to do if they
see the student has not

00:40:47.330 --> 00:40:48.190 align:middle line:90%
submitted an essay.

00:40:48.190 --> 00:40:51.250 align:middle line:90%


00:40:51.250 --> 00:40:55.433 align:middle line:84%
And if we think about
our experience with how

00:40:55.433 --> 00:40:56.850 align:middle line:84%
these things work,
what is a guess

00:40:56.850 --> 00:40:58.488 align:middle line:84%
about what the
school strategy might

00:40:58.488 --> 00:41:00.530 align:middle line:84%
be if we want to try to
look for this equilibrium

00:41:00.530 --> 00:41:02.690 align:middle line:84%
where all the students
are writing the essay.

00:41:02.690 --> 00:41:04.750 align:middle line:84%
What's your guess about
what the school might do?

00:41:04.750 --> 00:41:07.682 align:middle line:90%


00:41:07.682 --> 00:41:09.390 align:middle line:84%
If they see that you
submitted the essay,

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:11.650 align:middle line:90%
what are they going to do?

00:41:11.650 --> 00:41:13.450 align:middle line:84%
They're probably
going to accept you.

00:41:13.450 --> 00:41:15.503 align:middle line:84%
And if they see you
didn't write the essay,

00:41:15.503 --> 00:41:16.670 align:middle line:90%
they're going to reject you.

00:41:16.670 --> 00:41:19.468 align:middle line:90%
Let's see how this works out.

00:41:19.468 --> 00:41:21.010 align:middle line:84%
So first, we need
to say, well, let's

00:41:21.010 --> 00:41:23.450 align:middle line:84%
suppose they see that
you've written the essay.

00:41:23.450 --> 00:41:26.267 align:middle line:84%
What is the school believe about
your passion for the position.

00:41:26.267 --> 00:41:28.350 align:middle line:84%
Do they think you're
passionate or not passionate?

00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:29.710 align:middle line:90%
What probability do they assign?

00:41:29.710 --> 00:41:33.250 align:middle line:90%


00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:36.670 align:middle line:84%
1/2, 1/2, so technically,
we're using Bayes' rule,

00:41:36.670 --> 00:41:39.977 align:middle line:84%
but here it's pretty simple
because the prior is 1/2, 1/2.

00:41:39.977 --> 00:41:41.310 align:middle line:90%
1/2 the students are passionate.

00:41:41.310 --> 00:41:42.590 align:middle line:84%
1/2 the students
are not passionate.

00:41:42.590 --> 00:41:44.530 align:middle line:84%
If all the students
submit the essay, well,

00:41:44.530 --> 00:41:46.710 align:middle line:84%
then the students who submit the
essay are also 1/2 passionate,

00:41:46.710 --> 00:41:47.502 align:middle line:90%
1/2 not passionate.

00:41:47.502 --> 00:41:48.390 align:middle line:90%
Nothing has changed.

00:41:48.390 --> 00:41:51.722 align:middle line:90%


00:41:51.722 --> 00:41:53.930 align:middle line:84%
But what does the school
believe if they see that you

00:41:53.930 --> 00:41:55.070 align:middle line:90%
didn't submit the essay?

00:41:55.070 --> 00:41:57.177 align:middle line:90%


00:41:57.177 --> 00:41:58.510 align:middle line:90%
I don't know, what do you think.

00:41:58.510 --> 00:41:59.468 align:middle line:90%
This is a bit trickier.

00:41:59.468 --> 00:42:00.390 align:middle line:90%
Should it be 1/2, 1/2?

00:42:00.390 --> 00:42:01.090 align:middle line:90%
Why should it be 1/2, 1/2?

00:42:01.090 --> 00:42:02.070 align:middle line:90%
What do you think?

00:42:02.070 --> 00:42:04.928 align:middle line:90%


00:42:04.928 --> 00:42:06.470 align:middle line:84%
There's not one
right answer to this.

00:42:06.470 --> 00:42:09.130 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's unclear, but,
Amy, you said 1/2, 1/2, why?

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:13.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess the idea is
symmetric to the other one where

00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:14.250 align:middle line:90%
everyone's--

00:42:14.250 --> 00:42:17.370 align:middle line:90%
no one [INAUDIBLE] essay, but--

00:42:17.370 --> 00:42:18.250 align:middle line:90%
wait.

00:42:18.250 --> 00:42:20.505 align:middle line:84%
So if everyone submits
essay [INAUDIBLE] 1/2, 1/2.

00:42:20.505 --> 00:42:23.130 align:middle line:84%
And then that's like, you have
a student that you don't receive

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:27.970 align:middle line:84%
an essay from would also be
1/2, 1/2 because, basically,

00:42:27.970 --> 00:42:29.470 align:middle line:84%
the way that Bayes'
rule plays out,

00:42:29.470 --> 00:42:33.143 align:middle line:84%
they're all the same per
person for other cases.

00:42:33.143 --> 00:42:34.810 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: OK, so let's
maybe write out--

00:42:34.810 --> 00:42:36.410 align:middle line:84%
let's get another thought, and
then let's write out Bayes' rule

00:42:36.410 --> 00:42:37.493 align:middle line:90%
and see what happens here.

00:42:37.493 --> 00:42:40.250 align:middle line:84%
Were there any other
thoughts on what we do here?

00:42:40.250 --> 00:42:40.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:42:40.890 --> 00:42:42.690 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean, just like
qualitatively, then you

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:45.080 align:middle line:84%
assume that there's a greater
chance that they're not

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:46.320 align:middle line:90%
passionate about [INAUDIBLE].

00:42:46.320 --> 00:42:47.820 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: That also
seems reasonable.

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:49.987 align:middle line:84%
I think there's a lot of
reasonable answers to this,

00:42:49.987 --> 00:42:52.800 align:middle line:84%
but let's see how
Bayes' rule works.

00:42:52.800 --> 00:42:56.020 align:middle line:90%
So to apply Bayes' rule--

00:42:56.020 --> 00:43:00.312 align:middle line:90%


00:43:00.312 --> 00:43:02.682 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:02.682 --> 00:43:03.860 align:middle line:90%


00:43:03.860 --> 00:43:06.360 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yeah, so I think we're
going to have an issue when

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:07.235 align:middle line:90%
we apply Bayes' rule.

00:43:07.235 --> 00:43:09.560 align:middle line:90%
So let's see what happens.

00:43:09.560 --> 00:43:14.783 align:middle line:84%
So remember, just a quick
review of Bayes' rule.

00:43:14.783 --> 00:43:16.200 align:middle line:84%
Bayes' rule allows
us to calculate

00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:17.540 align:middle line:90%
conditional probabilities.

00:43:17.540 --> 00:43:21.100 align:middle line:84%
So it allows us to say something
like if we have two events, A

00:43:21.100 --> 00:43:24.680 align:middle line:84%
and B, what is the probability
that event A occurs

00:43:24.680 --> 00:43:27.332 align:middle line:90%
conditional on B occurring.

00:43:27.332 --> 00:43:29.040 align:middle line:84%
And the rule for Bayes'
rule is that this

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:34.600 align:middle line:84%
is the probability
of A and B occurring

00:43:34.600 --> 00:43:39.320 align:middle line:84%
divided by the probability
of B occurring.

00:43:39.320 --> 00:43:44.050 align:middle line:84%
And this basic picture
that we give, this is A,

00:43:44.050 --> 00:43:48.350 align:middle line:84%
and this is B. Well, what's the
probability of A occurring given

00:43:48.350 --> 00:43:48.850 align:middle line:90%
B?

00:43:48.850 --> 00:43:50.390 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we know
that B occurred,

00:43:50.390 --> 00:43:53.310 align:middle line:84%
we know we're somewhere
in this circle.

00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:55.870 align:middle line:84%
The probability that
A occurs given B

00:43:55.870 --> 00:43:57.667 align:middle line:84%
is saying what's
given that we're

00:43:57.667 --> 00:43:59.750 align:middle line:84%
in this circle B, what's
the likelihood that we're

00:43:59.750 --> 00:44:01.230 align:middle line:90%
in the shaded region.

00:44:01.230 --> 00:44:03.350 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly what's
being pictured here,

00:44:03.350 --> 00:44:05.870 align:middle line:84%
P of A intersect B
is the probability

00:44:05.870 --> 00:44:07.830 align:middle line:84%
that we're in this
shaded region.

00:44:07.830 --> 00:44:10.070 align:middle line:84%
And probability B is
just the probability

00:44:10.070 --> 00:44:14.770 align:middle line:84%
that we're in this
outer circle here.

00:44:14.770 --> 00:44:17.513 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to
apply Bayes' rule

00:44:17.513 --> 00:44:19.430 align:middle line:84%
when we see that the
student writes the essay.

00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:27.750 align:middle line:84%
So what we're interested
in is, given essay,

00:44:27.750 --> 00:44:31.550 align:middle line:84%
we want to say, what is the
probability that the student is

00:44:31.550 --> 00:44:34.410 align:middle line:84%
passionate, tP, given
that they write the essay?

00:44:34.410 --> 00:44:39.030 align:middle line:90%


00:44:39.030 --> 00:44:41.997 align:middle line:84%
And Bayes' rule tells us this
is equal to the probability

00:44:41.997 --> 00:44:43.580 align:middle line:84%
that they're passionate
and they write

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:45.517 align:middle line:84%
the essay divided
by the probability

00:44:45.517 --> 00:44:46.600 align:middle line:90%
that they write the essay.

00:44:46.600 --> 00:45:00.420 align:middle line:90%


00:45:00.420 --> 00:45:02.710 align:middle line:84%
Now, the probability
that they write the essay

00:45:02.710 --> 00:45:03.960 align:middle line:90%
is a little hard to calculate.

00:45:03.960 --> 00:45:06.620 align:middle line:90%
Let's break it down.

00:45:06.620 --> 00:45:08.027 align:middle line:90%
So write this one more time--

00:45:08.027 --> 00:45:10.360 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll write it over here
to give myself more space.

00:45:10.360 --> 00:45:12.540 align:middle line:84%
This is the probability
that they're passionate

00:45:12.540 --> 00:45:17.820 align:middle line:84%
and they write the
essay divided by-- well,

00:45:17.820 --> 00:45:19.660 align:middle line:84%
there's two ways they
could write the essay.

00:45:19.660 --> 00:45:21.780 align:middle line:84%
They could be passionate
and then write the essay,

00:45:21.780 --> 00:45:23.560 align:middle line:84%
or they could be not
passionate and write the essay.

00:45:23.560 --> 00:45:25.000 align:middle line:84%
So let me break
it down like that.

00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:30.685 align:middle line:84%
We have the probability
of tP and essay

00:45:30.685 --> 00:45:33.520 align:middle line:90%
plus the probability of tN.

00:45:33.520 --> 00:45:46.200 align:middle line:90%


00:45:46.200 --> 00:45:48.420 align:middle line:84%
So let's see if we
can fill this in.

00:45:48.420 --> 00:45:50.420 align:middle line:84%
What is the probability
that they're passionate,

00:45:50.420 --> 00:45:51.900 align:middle line:90%
and they write the essay?

00:45:51.900 --> 00:45:53.995 align:middle line:84%
Well, they always
write the essay.

00:45:53.995 --> 00:45:56.120 align:middle line:84%
So this is just the
probability they're passionate,

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:56.745 align:middle line:90%
and that's 1/2.

00:45:56.745 --> 00:46:00.420 align:middle line:90%


00:46:00.420 --> 00:46:05.180 align:middle line:84%
And on the bottom, again, we
have the 1/2 plus we're going

00:46:05.180 --> 00:46:07.690 align:middle line:84%
to get a second 1/2 because the
probability that they're not

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:09.440 align:middle line:84%
passionate and they
write the essay, well,

00:46:09.440 --> 00:46:11.820 align:middle line:84%
if they're not passionate,
they always write the essay.

00:46:11.820 --> 00:46:14.320 align:middle line:84%
So this is just the probability
that they're not passionate,

00:46:14.320 --> 00:46:15.620 align:middle line:90%
which is also 1/2.

00:46:15.620 --> 00:46:17.735 align:middle line:84%
So we get 1/2 divided
by 1/2 plus 1/2,

00:46:17.735 --> 00:46:18.860 align:middle line:90%
and that's going to be 1/2.

00:46:18.860 --> 00:46:22.020 align:middle line:90%


00:46:22.020 --> 00:46:24.640 align:middle line:84%
I think it's good to go
over this carefully once.

00:46:24.640 --> 00:46:26.480 align:middle line:84%
And then I think people
get the hang of it.

00:46:26.480 --> 00:46:29.180 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on this?

00:46:29.180 --> 00:46:30.280 align:middle line:90%
Two questions, OK, great.

00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:31.080 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:31.080 --> 00:46:32.100 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:34.780 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: This is
just confirming this.

00:46:34.780 --> 00:46:36.548 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yes.

00:46:36.548 --> 00:46:38.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So then
in the other case--

00:46:38.090 --> 00:46:41.930 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So let's go to
the other case, yeah, yeah.

00:46:41.930 --> 00:46:46.330 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll erase our little
review and go to the other case.

00:46:46.330 --> 00:46:49.142 align:middle line:84%
So now we want to say, given
that we see a student--

00:46:49.142 --> 00:46:50.350 align:middle line:90%
we didn't expect to see this.

00:46:50.350 --> 00:46:51.790 align:middle line:84%
We thought everyone was
supposed to write an essay.

00:46:51.790 --> 00:46:53.330 align:middle line:84%
That's what they're supposed
to do under their equilibrium

00:46:53.330 --> 00:46:54.150 align:middle line:90%
strategy.

00:46:54.150 --> 00:46:56.490 align:middle line:84%
But now we see that
someone submits

00:46:56.490 --> 00:46:59.305 align:middle line:90%
an application with no essay.

00:46:59.305 --> 00:47:00.930 align:middle line:84%
And we say, well,
what belief should we

00:47:00.930 --> 00:47:02.090 align:middle line:90%
form about this student?

00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:04.123 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's apply Bayes' rule.

00:47:04.123 --> 00:47:06.290 align:middle line:84%
We say, what's the probability
that they're actually

00:47:06.290 --> 00:47:09.230 align:middle line:84%
passionate, that I want to
accept them, given no essay?

00:47:09.230 --> 00:47:13.490 align:middle line:90%


00:47:13.490 --> 00:47:16.690 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly
equal to the probability

00:47:16.690 --> 00:47:25.016 align:middle line:84%
that they're
passionate and no essay

00:47:25.016 --> 00:47:27.190 align:middle line:84%
divided by the
probability of no essay.

00:47:27.190 --> 00:47:32.010 align:middle line:90%


00:47:32.010 --> 00:47:32.950 align:middle line:90%
And what is this?

00:47:32.950 --> 00:47:38.000 align:middle line:90%


00:47:38.000 --> 00:47:38.840 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:47:38.840 --> 00:47:39.740 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 0 over 0.

00:47:39.740 --> 00:47:41.200 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: This is 0 over 0.

00:47:41.200 --> 00:47:44.320 align:middle line:90%


00:47:44.320 --> 00:47:48.000 align:middle line:84%
Because I'm never-- no
one is writing no essay.

00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:49.208 align:middle line:90%
Everyone is writing an essay.

00:47:49.208 --> 00:47:50.917 align:middle line:84%
So the probability
that you're passionate

00:47:50.917 --> 00:47:53.740 align:middle line:84%
and you don't write an essay is
0 because if you're passionate,

00:47:53.740 --> 00:47:54.982 align:middle line:90%
you always write an essay.

00:47:54.982 --> 00:47:56.940 align:middle line:84%
The probability that you
don't submit an essay,

00:47:56.940 --> 00:47:59.440 align:middle line:84%
well, if you're passionate,
you always submit an essay.

00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:01.648 align:middle line:84%
If you're not passionate,
you always submit an essay.

00:48:01.648 --> 00:48:04.240 align:middle line:90%
So we get a 0 here as well.

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:06.600 align:middle line:84%
So what we see is that
there's a fundamental problem

00:48:06.600 --> 00:48:08.800 align:middle line:84%
with Bayes' rule,
that Bayes' rule only

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:11.160 align:middle line:84%
allows us to define
conditional probabilities

00:48:11.160 --> 00:48:14.200 align:middle line:84%
conditional on events that
have positive probability.

00:48:14.200 --> 00:48:16.240 align:middle line:84%
When we try to define
conditional probabilities

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:18.200 align:middle line:84%
conditioned on 0
probability events,

00:48:18.200 --> 00:48:20.960 align:middle line:84%
Bayes' rule doesn't really
apply, and we run into problems.

00:48:20.960 --> 00:48:23.340 align:middle line:90%
So I'll say not well defined.

00:48:23.340 --> 00:48:28.040 align:middle line:90%


00:48:28.040 --> 00:48:30.800 align:middle line:84%
And I think this is exactly why
we got a few different answers

00:48:30.800 --> 00:48:31.400 align:middle line:90%
here.

00:48:31.400 --> 00:48:33.690 align:middle line:84%
What should we conclude when
someone doesn't write an essay?

00:48:33.690 --> 00:48:35.470 align:middle line:84%
I think there's a few
reasonable choices.

00:48:35.470 --> 00:48:38.130 align:middle line:84%
You might say, well, this
isn't supposed to happen.

00:48:38.130 --> 00:48:40.990 align:middle line:84%
So let me just go back to my
prior and say it's 1/2, 1/2.

00:48:40.990 --> 00:48:42.710 align:middle line:84%
You could say, well,
the fact that they

00:48:42.710 --> 00:48:44.350 align:middle line:84%
don't want to write
an essay, that

00:48:44.350 --> 00:48:46.250 align:middle line:84%
must mean they're not really
passionate about the program.

00:48:46.250 --> 00:48:46.790 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to assume--

00:48:46.790 --> 00:48:48.290 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to say that
they're not passionate.

00:48:48.290 --> 00:48:50.582 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe you think they are
passionate, and they just--

00:48:50.582 --> 00:48:52.810 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, you could
tell a lot of stories.

00:48:52.810 --> 00:48:57.630 align:middle line:84%
But basically, we don't really
pin down what the belief is.

00:48:57.630 --> 00:49:01.050 align:middle line:84%
But now this creates a problem
because now the question is--

00:49:01.050 --> 00:49:04.310 align:middle line:90%


00:49:04.310 --> 00:49:07.130 align:middle line:84%
so this equilibrium
seems to fit.

00:49:07.130 --> 00:49:08.750 align:middle line:90%
Let's see if this makes sense.

00:49:08.750 --> 00:49:11.910 align:middle line:84%
If I'm the student, if I
believe that the school is going

00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:14.870 align:middle line:84%
to accept me if I write
an essay and not accept me

00:49:14.870 --> 00:49:17.870 align:middle line:84%
if I don't write the essay,
then it's optimal for me

00:49:17.870 --> 00:49:20.030 align:middle line:90%
to write the essay.

00:49:20.030 --> 00:49:21.500 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

00:49:21.500 --> 00:49:23.750 align:middle line:84%
And given that all the
students are writing the essay,

00:49:23.750 --> 00:49:26.310 align:middle line:84%
well, the school is
happy to accept you

00:49:26.310 --> 00:49:29.030 align:middle line:84%
because the school
gets a payoff of 3

00:49:29.030 --> 00:49:31.850 align:middle line:84%
from accepting a
passionate student,

00:49:31.850 --> 00:49:34.190 align:middle line:84%
negative 2 from accepting
a not passionate student,

00:49:34.190 --> 00:49:37.110 align:middle line:84%
And the average of 3 and
negative 2 is strictly positive.

00:49:37.110 --> 00:49:38.530 align:middle line:90%
So everything looks good here.

00:49:38.530 --> 00:49:41.530 align:middle line:84%
The only question is, is
this a credible threat?

00:49:41.530 --> 00:49:49.643 align:middle line:90%


00:49:49.643 --> 00:49:50.810 align:middle line:90%
But there's a question here.

00:49:50.810 --> 00:49:51.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:49:51.550 --> 00:49:53.070 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I was just curious.

00:49:53.070 --> 00:49:56.050 align:middle line:84%
If you were to get someone
who submits an essay,

00:49:56.050 --> 00:49:59.150 align:middle line:84%
could that also imply that maybe
there's a third type of student

00:49:59.150 --> 00:50:00.360 align:middle line:90%
that you don't know of?

00:50:00.360 --> 00:50:01.610 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: That's a good point.

00:50:01.610 --> 00:50:03.777 align:middle line:84%
So maybe you want to revise
your model of the world.

00:50:03.777 --> 00:50:06.430 align:middle line:84%
And maybe our types, we've
only represented types

00:50:06.430 --> 00:50:08.370 align:middle line:84%
by how excited they
are about the program,

00:50:08.370 --> 00:50:09.953 align:middle line:84%
but maybe there's a
laziness component

00:50:09.953 --> 00:50:11.450 align:middle line:90%
that we also need to model.

00:50:11.450 --> 00:50:13.590 align:middle line:84%
Or-- and we'll talk
about this later--

00:50:13.590 --> 00:50:17.070 align:middle line:84%
it could be that this cost
c varies in the population.

00:50:17.070 --> 00:50:18.490 align:middle line:84%
Again, talking
about essay costs,

00:50:18.490 --> 00:50:20.290 align:middle line:84%
I feel like it's 0 for
everyone in the world of GPT,

00:50:20.290 --> 00:50:22.053 align:middle line:84%
but in the old days,
there was a cost.

00:50:22.053 --> 00:50:24.470 align:middle line:84%
And some people were better
at writing essays and the cost

00:50:24.470 --> 00:50:27.012 align:middle line:84%
for them was lower, and we're
going to talk about that later.

00:50:27.012 --> 00:50:28.430 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, exactly.

00:50:28.430 --> 00:50:30.540 align:middle line:84%
So I guess the
basic question here

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:32.640 align:middle line:90%
is, is this a credible threat?

00:50:32.640 --> 00:50:34.940 align:middle line:84%
Is it credible for
the school to say,

00:50:34.940 --> 00:50:37.837 align:middle line:84%
as they do in this equilibrium,
if you don't submit an essay,

00:50:37.837 --> 00:50:38.920 align:middle line:90%
we're going to reject you?

00:50:38.920 --> 00:50:41.840 align:middle line:90%


00:50:41.840 --> 00:50:42.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:50:42.340 --> 00:50:43.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, isn't
it kind of murky

00:50:43.460 --> 00:50:45.160 align:middle line:84%
because, as you were
just talking about,

00:50:45.160 --> 00:50:46.880 align:middle line:84%
there are some ways
you could just say,

00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:48.530 align:middle line:84%
if you don't write an essay
that you're not passionate,

00:50:48.530 --> 00:50:49.600 align:middle line:90%
so we don't want you.

00:50:49.600 --> 00:50:51.700 align:middle line:84%
But what if the school
has a really different way

00:50:51.700 --> 00:50:53.280 align:middle line:84%
of thinking like, oh, he
didn't write the essay.

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:55.947 align:middle line:84%
That's because you were spending
more time studying for an exam.

00:50:55.947 --> 00:50:58.040 align:middle line:84%
So then we're
actually more excited,

00:50:58.040 --> 00:50:59.420 align:middle line:84%
so I think because
you don't know

00:50:59.420 --> 00:51:02.900 align:middle line:84%
exactly what the school will
conclude, the threat is--

00:51:02.900 --> 00:51:04.100 align:middle line:90%
it's not necessarily--

00:51:04.100 --> 00:51:05.000 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: I think
that's a great answer.

00:51:05.000 --> 00:51:06.000 align:middle line:90%
So it's definitely murky.

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:06.940 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly
what I'm trying

00:51:06.940 --> 00:51:08.880 align:middle line:84%
to convey with this
example, that it is murky.

00:51:08.880 --> 00:51:11.020 align:middle line:84%
But I think the key point,
which you hinted at,

00:51:11.020 --> 00:51:15.060 align:middle line:84%
was whether this threat
is credible depends

00:51:15.060 --> 00:51:18.500 align:middle line:84%
on what the school believes
when they don't see an essay.

00:51:18.500 --> 00:51:19.900 align:middle line:90%
So is this threat credible?

00:51:19.900 --> 00:51:21.560 align:middle line:90%
Well, it depends on the belief.

00:51:21.560 --> 00:51:27.970 align:middle line:90%


00:51:27.970 --> 00:51:29.530 align:middle line:84%
If the school
believes that everyone

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:31.645 align:middle line:84%
who doesn't submit an
essay is passionate,

00:51:31.645 --> 00:51:33.270 align:middle line:84%
then it's not credible
for them to say,

00:51:33.270 --> 00:51:35.687 align:middle line:84%
we're going to reject those
students because they'd rather

00:51:35.687 --> 00:51:37.370 align:middle line:84%
accept passionate
students than not.

00:51:37.370 --> 00:51:39.770 align:middle line:84%
But if their belief,
after not seeing an essay,

00:51:39.770 --> 00:51:42.725 align:middle line:84%
is that the student
is not passionate,

00:51:42.725 --> 00:51:44.850 align:middle line:84%
then it is credible for
them to reject that student

00:51:44.850 --> 00:51:46.750 align:middle line:84%
because that's exactly
what's optimal for them,

00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:49.370 align:middle line:84%
if they see that a student-- if
they believe that a student is

00:51:49.370 --> 00:51:51.210 align:middle line:90%
not passionate.

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:52.770 align:middle line:90%
So how do we get around this?

00:51:52.770 --> 00:51:55.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, beliefs
aren't well defined,

00:51:55.530 --> 00:51:58.410 align:middle line:84%
but whether something is
credible depends on the belief.

00:51:58.410 --> 00:52:01.610 align:middle line:84%
The way we get at this
is our equilibrium notion

00:52:01.610 --> 00:52:04.010 align:middle line:84%
is going to need to
explicitly specify

00:52:04.010 --> 00:52:09.210 align:middle line:84%
what beliefs the school forms,
even at this information set.

00:52:09.210 --> 00:52:12.630 align:middle line:84%
So as a result, beliefs must
be part of the equilibrium.

00:52:12.630 --> 00:52:22.690 align:middle line:90%


00:52:22.690 --> 00:52:23.890 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:52:23.890 --> 00:52:27.730 align:middle line:84%
Well, specifying
strategies alone

00:52:27.730 --> 00:52:29.792 align:middle line:84%
is not enough to tell
us what people believe.

00:52:29.792 --> 00:52:31.250 align:middle line:84%
But our whole
equilibrium notion is

00:52:31.250 --> 00:52:33.370 align:middle line:84%
supposed to capture whether
these threats are credible.

00:52:33.370 --> 00:52:35.703 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, we're going
to need to explicitly specify

00:52:35.703 --> 00:52:37.210 align:middle line:90%
what the belief is.

00:52:37.210 --> 00:52:40.650 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll see here
is that, here, there's

00:52:40.650 --> 00:52:43.350 align:middle line:84%
no flexibility in what
belief we specify here.

00:52:43.350 --> 00:52:45.670 align:middle line:84%
In this case, the belief is
pinned down by Bayes' rule.

00:52:45.670 --> 00:52:48.290 align:middle line:84%
Bayes' rule tells us what
the belief has to be.

00:52:48.290 --> 00:52:51.150 align:middle line:84%
Here, we can choose
whatever belief we want.

00:52:51.150 --> 00:52:53.970 align:middle line:84%
There's no further restriction
imposed by Bayes' rule

00:52:53.970 --> 00:52:56.010 align:middle line:84%
on what the school's
belief is here.

00:52:56.010 --> 00:52:58.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, I said before,
there's all this big debate

00:52:58.090 --> 00:53:00.173 align:middle line:84%
in the literature
about how to define it.

00:53:00.173 --> 00:53:02.090 align:middle line:84%
People came along and
made all these arguments

00:53:02.090 --> 00:53:04.702 align:middle line:84%
about what belief should be at
this kind of information set.

00:53:04.702 --> 00:53:05.910 align:middle line:90%
What should you believe here?

00:53:05.910 --> 00:53:08.035 align:middle line:84%
For now, we're not going
to impose any restrictions

00:53:08.035 --> 00:53:09.210 align:middle line:90%
on what those beliefs are.

00:53:09.210 --> 00:53:14.290 align:middle line:84%
And now I'm going to go
into the formal definition.

00:53:14.290 --> 00:53:15.910 align:middle line:90%
Let's go back over here.

00:53:15.910 --> 00:53:30.560 align:middle line:90%


00:53:30.560 --> 00:53:38.020 align:middle line:84%
Let's define perfect
Bayesian equilibrium.

00:53:38.020 --> 00:53:43.280 align:middle line:90%


00:53:43.280 --> 00:53:45.080 align:middle line:84%
So the first key point
is that one thing

00:53:45.080 --> 00:53:47.020 align:middle line:84%
I've harped on throughout the
semester is no longer true.

00:53:47.020 --> 00:53:48.603 align:middle line:84%
So throughout the
semester, I've said,

00:53:48.603 --> 00:53:50.180 align:middle line:84%
an equilibrium is
a strategy profile.

00:53:50.180 --> 00:53:51.960 align:middle line:84%
And I said this
over and over again.

00:53:51.960 --> 00:53:54.320 align:middle line:84%
Now, that's no longer
true because equilibrium

00:53:54.320 --> 00:53:56.937 align:middle line:84%
is going to be a strategy
profile together with beliefs.

00:53:56.937 --> 00:53:59.520 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to have to have
this new object, which is going

00:53:59.520 --> 00:54:01.020 align:middle line:90%
to be called an assessment.

00:54:01.020 --> 00:54:03.497 align:middle line:90%


00:54:03.497 --> 00:54:05.080 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a
silly word, but this

00:54:05.080 --> 00:54:06.400 align:middle line:90%
is the word that's used.

00:54:06.400 --> 00:54:08.520 align:middle line:90%
And that specifies two things.

00:54:08.520 --> 00:54:10.540 align:middle line:90%
It specifies a strategy profile.

00:54:10.540 --> 00:54:14.880 align:middle line:90%


00:54:14.880 --> 00:54:18.020 align:middle line:84%
And also beliefs, sometimes
it's called a belief system.

00:54:18.020 --> 00:54:25.310 align:middle line:90%


00:54:25.310 --> 00:54:28.410 align:middle line:84%
So strategy profile, we
already know what that is.

00:54:28.410 --> 00:54:30.210 align:middle line:84%
It specifies a strategy
for each player.

00:54:30.210 --> 00:54:32.910 align:middle line:84%
And a strategy in this
game and in any game

00:54:32.910 --> 00:54:35.810 align:middle line:84%
is a mapping from
information sets to actions.

00:54:35.810 --> 00:54:37.310 align:middle line:84%
It's a complete
contingent plan that

00:54:37.310 --> 00:54:39.560 align:middle line:84%
says that my information set
and any of my information

00:54:39.560 --> 00:54:41.150 align:middle line:90%
sets, what am I going to do?

00:54:41.150 --> 00:54:45.270 align:middle line:84%
So in this game, it's exactly
what we wrote down over here.

00:54:45.270 --> 00:54:49.310 align:middle line:84%
The student has two
information sets tP and tN.

00:54:49.310 --> 00:54:52.430 align:middle line:84%
So a strategy for the student
specifies what they do at tP

00:54:52.430 --> 00:54:54.190 align:middle line:90%
and what they do at tN.

00:54:54.190 --> 00:54:56.327 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, the school
has two information sets.

00:54:56.327 --> 00:54:57.910 align:middle line:84%
The information set
where they observe

00:54:57.910 --> 00:54:59.827 align:middle line:84%
the student wrote an
essay and the information

00:54:59.827 --> 00:55:02.243 align:middle line:84%
set where they observed the
student didn't write an essay,

00:55:02.243 --> 00:55:03.910 align:middle line:84%
and a strategy
specifies what action

00:55:03.910 --> 00:55:08.030 align:middle line:84%
they take at each of
those informations.

00:55:08.030 --> 00:55:09.310 align:middle line:90%
So I said that in words.

00:55:09.310 --> 00:55:13.550 align:middle line:84%
Now, a belief system
is going to specify--

00:55:13.550 --> 00:55:22.172 align:middle line:90%


00:55:22.172 --> 00:55:34.980 align:middle line:84%
specifies a belief at every
non-singleton information set.

00:55:34.980 --> 00:55:37.340 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of the information
sets are singletons.

00:55:37.340 --> 00:55:38.960 align:middle line:84%
I don't need to
specify a belief here.

00:55:38.960 --> 00:55:41.820 align:middle line:84%
I only need to specify a belief
at an information set that

00:55:41.820 --> 00:55:43.700 align:middle line:90%
contains multiple nodes.

00:55:43.700 --> 00:55:44.918 align:middle line:90%
And what is a belief?

00:55:44.918 --> 00:55:46.460 align:middle line:84%
A belief is going
to be a probability

00:55:46.460 --> 00:55:49.700 align:middle line:84%
distribution over the nodes
in that information set.

00:55:49.700 --> 00:55:51.220 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly
what we did here.

00:55:51.220 --> 00:55:55.540 align:middle line:84%
We said if the student
submitted an essay,

00:55:55.540 --> 00:55:57.520 align:middle line:84%
we had two nodes in
that information set.

00:55:57.520 --> 00:55:59.100 align:middle line:84%
And we had to form
belief about what

00:55:59.100 --> 00:56:01.380 align:middle line:84%
was the relative likelihood
of those two nodes.

00:56:01.380 --> 00:56:03.860 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, at this
information set, no essay--

00:56:03.860 --> 00:56:05.080 align:middle line:90%
or maybe I got it backwards.

00:56:05.080 --> 00:56:06.820 align:middle line:84%
At the essay
information set, we have

00:56:06.820 --> 00:56:09.140 align:middle line:84%
to form a belief
about the two nodes

00:56:09.140 --> 00:56:10.640 align:middle line:90%
within that information set.

00:56:10.640 --> 00:56:13.000 align:middle line:84%
So a belief at every
non-singleton information set.

00:56:13.000 --> 00:56:14.460 align:middle line:90%
What is a belief?

00:56:14.460 --> 00:56:28.000 align:middle line:84%
i.e., a probability
distribution over-- well,

00:56:28.000 --> 00:56:29.125 align:middle line:90%
it can't be over all nodes.

00:56:29.125 --> 00:56:31.000 align:middle line:84%
I certainly can't assign
positive probability

00:56:31.000 --> 00:56:32.920 align:middle line:84%
to nodes that are not
in that information set.

00:56:32.920 --> 00:56:34.660 align:middle line:84%
It's only a distribution
over the nodes that are

00:56:34.660 --> 00:56:35.960 align:middle line:90%
within that information set.

00:56:35.960 --> 00:56:37.377 align:middle line:84%
So it's a probability
distribution

00:56:37.377 --> 00:56:40.640 align:middle line:84%
over nodes in the
information set.

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:50.700 align:middle line:90%


00:56:50.700 --> 00:56:52.160 align:middle line:84%
So now we've defined
an assessment.

00:56:52.160 --> 00:56:55.420 align:middle line:84%
Let's formally define a
perfect Bayesian equilibrium.

00:56:55.420 --> 00:57:04.460 align:middle line:84%
So definition, an
assessment is a PBE

00:57:04.460 --> 00:57:06.400 align:middle line:90%
if it satisfies two conditions.

00:57:06.400 --> 00:57:11.180 align:middle line:90%


00:57:11.180 --> 00:57:14.120 align:middle line:84%
The first condition is
sequential rationality.

00:57:14.120 --> 00:57:21.010 align:middle line:90%


00:57:21.010 --> 00:57:22.950 align:middle line:84%
And the second is
belief consistency.

00:57:22.950 --> 00:57:31.850 align:middle line:90%


00:57:31.850 --> 00:57:33.350 align:middle line:84%
Let's start with
belief consistency.

00:57:33.350 --> 00:57:34.810 align:middle line:84%
This is what we're
getting at here.

00:57:34.810 --> 00:57:39.210 align:middle line:84%
We want to say that the school's
belief here must be 1/2, 1/2.

00:57:39.210 --> 00:57:42.050 align:middle line:84%
But the school's belief
here is totally flexible.

00:57:42.050 --> 00:57:44.830 align:middle line:84%
So what belief
consistency says is,

00:57:44.830 --> 00:57:46.690 align:middle line:84%
well, the beliefs
are consistent.

00:57:46.690 --> 00:57:51.070 align:middle line:84%
The beliefs are consistent
with Bayes' rule.

00:57:51.070 --> 00:57:58.770 align:middle line:90%


00:57:58.770 --> 00:58:02.030 align:middle line:84%
Well, they can't be consistent
with Bayes' rule everywhere.

00:58:02.030 --> 00:58:05.150 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes we use this vague
phrase wherever possible.

00:58:05.150 --> 00:58:12.890 align:middle line:90%


00:58:12.890 --> 00:58:15.560 align:middle line:84%
There's often different ways of
defining exactly what wherever

00:58:15.560 --> 00:58:16.460 align:middle line:90%
possible means.

00:58:16.460 --> 00:58:19.520 align:middle line:84%
What I mean by wherever possible
is at any information set that's

00:58:19.520 --> 00:58:25.340 align:middle line:84%
reached with positive
probability, so i.e.

00:58:25.340 --> 00:58:33.560 align:middle line:90%


00:58:33.560 --> 00:58:35.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's be careful--
the probability

00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:39.220 align:middle line:84%
that we reach an information set
depends on the strategy profile.

00:58:39.220 --> 00:58:42.760 align:middle line:84%
So what we're saying is we
have an assessment, looking

00:58:42.760 --> 00:58:45.080 align:middle line:84%
at the strategy profile that's
part of that assessment,

00:58:45.080 --> 00:58:46.540 align:middle line:90%
I can go every information set.

00:58:46.540 --> 00:58:48.160 align:middle line:84%
And I can compute
the probability

00:58:48.160 --> 00:58:49.785 align:middle line:84%
that we actually
reach that information

00:58:49.785 --> 00:58:51.360 align:middle line:90%
set under the strategy profile.

00:58:51.360 --> 00:58:53.503 align:middle line:84%
And I can split those
information sets in two.

00:58:53.503 --> 00:58:54.920 align:middle line:84%
I can say there's
some information

00:58:54.920 --> 00:58:56.860 align:middle line:84%
sets that are reached
with positive probability.

00:58:56.860 --> 00:58:58.640 align:middle line:84%
And there are some
that are never reached.

00:58:58.640 --> 00:59:00.820 align:middle line:84%
And the ones that are reached
with positive probability,

00:59:00.820 --> 00:59:02.520 align:middle line:84%
the beliefs that
those information sets

00:59:02.520 --> 00:59:04.425 align:middle line:90%
must satisfy Bayes' rule.

00:59:04.425 --> 00:59:05.800 align:middle line:84%
But at the other
information sets

00:59:05.800 --> 00:59:08.040 align:middle line:84%
that are not reached with
positive probability,

00:59:08.040 --> 00:59:11.880 align:middle line:84%
I have total flexibility
over those beliefs.

00:59:11.880 --> 00:59:14.390 align:middle line:84%
There's no restriction
on the beliefs there.

00:59:14.390 --> 00:59:17.710 align:middle line:90%
So now sequential rationality.

00:59:17.710 --> 00:59:18.750 align:middle line:90%
We're close.

00:59:18.750 --> 00:59:22.430 align:middle line:84%
Well, the whole point
of introducing beliefs

00:59:22.430 --> 00:59:25.630 align:middle line:84%
was to determine whether
these threats were credible.

00:59:25.630 --> 00:59:28.810 align:middle line:84%
And what credible meant,
precisely, was optimal.

00:59:28.810 --> 00:59:32.070 align:middle line:84%
Optimal given my beliefs and
given the strategy of others.

00:59:32.070 --> 00:59:39.710 align:middle line:84%
So sequential rationality
means, at every information set,

00:59:39.710 --> 00:59:46.910 align:middle line:84%
the action under the
strategy profile is optimal,

00:59:46.910 --> 00:59:56.970 align:middle line:84%
given beliefs and the rest
of the strategy profile.

00:59:56.970 --> 01:00:00.470 align:middle line:90%


01:00:00.470 --> 01:00:02.970 align:middle line:90%
And let me say the rest.

01:00:02.970 --> 01:00:12.710 align:middle line:90%


01:00:12.710 --> 01:00:14.870 align:middle line:90%
Put this in quotes.

01:00:14.870 --> 01:00:16.750 align:middle line:90%
So what do I mean here?

01:00:16.750 --> 01:00:18.950 align:middle line:84%
Whenever we get to
an information set,

01:00:18.950 --> 01:00:21.030 align:middle line:84%
a strategy is going
to specify what

01:00:21.030 --> 01:00:25.023 align:middle line:84%
action the player is supposed
to take at that information set.

01:00:25.023 --> 01:00:27.190 align:middle line:84%
And that action that they're
supposed to take better

01:00:27.190 --> 01:00:28.142 align:middle line:90%
be optimal.

01:00:28.142 --> 01:00:29.850 align:middle line:84%
Well, how do we define
that it's optimal?

01:00:29.850 --> 01:00:33.230 align:middle line:84%
Well, we have to check that it's
optimal given the beliefs that

01:00:33.230 --> 01:00:37.430 align:middle line:84%
the player has there and how
everyone else is going to play,

01:00:37.430 --> 01:00:39.990 align:middle line:84%
which is the "rest of
the strategy profile."

01:00:39.990 --> 01:00:41.830 align:middle line:84%
So what's optimal
for me at this node

01:00:41.830 --> 01:00:44.390 align:middle line:84%
depends on-- or at
this information set

01:00:44.390 --> 01:00:46.030 align:middle line:84%
depends on my beliefs
over which node

01:00:46.030 --> 01:00:49.150 align:middle line:84%
I'm actually at and also, how
people are going to subsequently

01:00:49.150 --> 01:00:50.190 align:middle line:90%
play in the game.

01:00:50.190 --> 01:00:53.190 align:middle line:84%
And the way they subsequently
play is pinned down by the "rest

01:00:53.190 --> 01:00:54.548 align:middle line:90%
of the strategy profile."

01:00:54.548 --> 01:00:57.090 align:middle line:84%
I could write all this in math,
and that's what the notes do,

01:00:57.090 --> 01:00:58.070 align:middle line:84%
but I think that
maybe just makes

01:00:58.070 --> 01:00:59.210 align:middle line:90%
things a little more confusing.

01:00:59.210 --> 01:01:00.668 align:middle line:84%
So I'm trying to
write it in words,

01:01:00.668 --> 01:01:02.610 align:middle line:84%
but if anything's
unclear, let me know.

01:01:02.610 --> 01:01:06.830 align:middle line:90%


01:01:06.830 --> 01:01:09.238 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

01:01:09.238 --> 01:01:10.780 align:middle line:84%
So let's go back to
this game and see

01:01:10.780 --> 01:01:17.500 align:middle line:90%
if we can find some more PBE.

01:01:17.500 --> 01:01:20.320 align:middle line:90%
So let's look here.

01:01:20.320 --> 01:01:36.620 align:middle line:90%


01:01:36.620 --> 01:01:38.700 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to finish
off our construction

01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:40.620 align:middle line:84%
of a perfect Bayesian
equilibrium over here.

01:01:40.620 --> 01:01:43.740 align:middle line:84%
We already have a
strategy for the student.

01:01:43.740 --> 01:01:45.900 align:middle line:84%
We already have a
strategy for the school.

01:01:45.900 --> 01:01:48.820 align:middle line:84%
The next step is we
have to specify beliefs.

01:01:48.820 --> 01:01:51.140 align:middle line:84%
We've already specified
the 1/2, 1/2 belief.

01:01:51.140 --> 01:01:55.420 align:middle line:84%
But we have flexibility
over the p1 minus p belief.

01:01:55.420 --> 01:01:58.300 align:middle line:84%
So let's emphasize
here that this is

01:01:58.300 --> 01:01:59.920 align:middle line:90%
unconstrained by Bayes' rule.

01:01:59.920 --> 01:02:08.090 align:middle line:90%


01:02:08.090 --> 01:02:10.730 align:middle line:84%
Now one confusion I often
see on problem sets or exams

01:02:10.730 --> 01:02:13.090 align:middle line:84%
is people say, oh,
here's my equilibrium.

01:02:13.090 --> 01:02:16.370 align:middle line:84%
You can take p
whatever you want.

01:02:16.370 --> 01:02:18.890 align:middle line:84%
Just because it's
unconstrained by Bayes' rule,

01:02:18.890 --> 01:02:20.850 align:middle line:84%
to write down an
equilibrium, we still

01:02:20.850 --> 01:02:22.730 align:middle line:84%
have to make a choice
about what this is.

01:02:22.730 --> 01:02:24.710 align:middle line:84%
We have a lot of flexibility
in what that choice is,

01:02:24.710 --> 01:02:26.585 align:middle line:84%
but you still have to
make a concrete choice.

01:02:26.585 --> 01:02:28.930 align:middle line:84%
You can't say the equilibrium
is whatever p you want.

01:02:28.930 --> 01:02:30.685 align:middle line:84%
You have to choose
a particular P.

01:02:30.685 --> 01:02:32.310 align:middle line:84%
And that's what we're
going to do here.

01:02:32.310 --> 01:02:35.010 align:middle line:84%
So let's see what
p we can choose.

01:02:35.010 --> 01:02:38.050 align:middle line:84%
So this p is unconstrained
by Bayes' rule,

01:02:38.050 --> 01:02:40.130 align:middle line:84%
but it is constrained
by something else.

01:02:40.130 --> 01:02:42.350 align:middle line:84%
It is constrained by
Sequential Rationality.

01:02:42.350 --> 01:02:48.930 align:middle line:90%


01:02:48.930 --> 01:02:49.810 align:middle line:90%
Say Sr.

01:02:49.810 --> 01:02:51.050 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:02:51.050 --> 01:02:53.830 align:middle line:84%
Why am I not allowed to
choose whatever p I want here?

01:02:53.830 --> 01:02:56.170 align:middle line:84%
So I want to make
this construct here

01:02:56.170 --> 01:02:58.048 align:middle line:90%
a perfect Bayesian equilibrium.

01:02:58.048 --> 01:03:00.590 align:middle line:84%
But you might think, oh, I can
just choose whatever P I want,

01:03:00.590 --> 01:03:01.870 align:middle line:90%
but I think there's an issue.

01:03:01.870 --> 01:03:02.730 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:03:02.730 --> 01:03:05.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean, if
you were to accept--

01:03:05.210 --> 01:03:10.160 align:middle line:84%
or to assume that it was more
passionate and not passionate

01:03:10.160 --> 01:03:15.200 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] since you would
be rejecting more [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:15.200 --> 01:03:17.560 align:middle line:84%
likely would've been a
higher utility [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:17.560 --> 01:03:18.560 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

01:03:18.560 --> 01:03:21.600 align:middle line:84%
So let's check whether rejecting
is sequentially rational given

01:03:21.600 --> 01:03:23.200 align:middle line:90%
this belief.

01:03:23.200 --> 01:03:27.400 align:middle line:84%
So my payoff as the school
is always 0 if I reject.

01:03:27.400 --> 01:03:34.060 align:middle line:84%
So my payoff from
rejecting is 0.

01:03:34.060 --> 01:03:36.760 align:middle line:90%


01:03:36.760 --> 01:03:40.170 align:middle line:84%
What's my expected payoff from
accepting given this belief?

01:03:40.170 --> 01:03:42.420 align:middle line:84%
My expected payoff of accepting
depends on the belief.

01:03:42.420 --> 01:03:43.800 align:middle line:90%
So let's write it down.

01:03:43.800 --> 01:03:46.800 align:middle line:84%
So we have-- well,
p is the probability

01:03:46.800 --> 01:03:49.440 align:middle line:90%
that the student is passionate.

01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:51.240 align:middle line:84%
And if they're
passionate, I think

01:03:51.240 --> 01:03:54.240 align:middle line:90%
we said I get a payoff of 3.

01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:57.080 align:middle line:90%


01:03:57.080 --> 01:04:03.520 align:middle line:84%
And if they're not passionate,
I get a payoff of negative 2.

01:04:03.520 --> 01:04:06.880 align:middle line:84%
So in order for rejecting
to be sequentially

01:04:06.880 --> 01:04:08.960 align:middle line:84%
rational at this
node, it must be

01:04:08.960 --> 01:04:10.640 align:middle line:84%
that the expected
payoff from rejecting

01:04:10.640 --> 01:04:14.560 align:middle line:84%
is weakly higher than the
expected payoff from accepting.

01:04:14.560 --> 01:04:18.280 align:middle line:84%
So I need 0 to be greater
than or equal to this.

01:04:18.280 --> 01:04:22.520 align:middle line:84%
And now let's simplify
this a little bit.

01:04:22.520 --> 01:04:34.400 align:middle line:84%
We get 3p, and then
we get 5p minus 2.

01:04:34.400 --> 01:04:35.440 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

01:04:35.440 --> 01:04:38.240 align:middle line:84%
So we need 0 greater than
or equal to 5p minus 2.

01:04:38.240 --> 01:04:42.480 align:middle line:84%
Let's put the 2 over here
and then divide by 5.

01:04:42.480 --> 01:04:48.260 align:middle line:84%
So we must get p less
than or equal to 2/5.

01:04:48.260 --> 01:04:50.880 align:middle line:90%


01:04:50.880 --> 01:04:52.840 align:middle line:84%
So we've actually
constructed a family

01:04:52.840 --> 01:04:54.520 align:middle line:90%
of perfect Bayesian equilibrium.

01:04:54.520 --> 01:04:57.360 align:middle line:84%
For any p that you give me, as
long as that p is smaller than

01:04:57.360 --> 01:05:00.960 align:middle line:84%
2/5, we have a perfect Bayesian
equilibria where the student

01:05:00.960 --> 01:05:04.230 align:middle line:84%
always submits the essay, the
school accepts if you submit

01:05:04.230 --> 01:05:06.590 align:middle line:84%
an essay, they reject if
you don't submit an essay,

01:05:06.590 --> 01:05:08.590 align:middle line:84%
and if they see that you
don't submit an essay,

01:05:08.590 --> 01:05:11.950 align:middle line:84%
they assign belief p to you
being a passionate student.

01:05:11.950 --> 01:05:13.830 align:middle line:84%
And that belief p
must be small enough

01:05:13.830 --> 01:05:16.690 align:middle line:84%
that it's credible for
them to actually reject.

01:05:16.690 --> 01:05:17.215 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:05:17.215 --> 01:05:18.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So does
that mean that,

01:05:18.590 --> 01:05:22.750 align:middle line:84%
given different students,
as long as they can assign

01:05:22.750 --> 01:05:25.150 align:middle line:90%
one person 3 and one person--

01:05:25.150 --> 01:05:27.927 align:middle line:84%
like, different students
who don't submit essays

01:05:27.927 --> 01:05:29.510 align:middle line:84%
can have different
values of p as long

01:05:29.510 --> 01:05:31.447 align:middle line:90%
as it just doesn't make sense.

01:05:31.447 --> 01:05:33.030 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So I think
you're describing

01:05:33.030 --> 01:05:35.023 align:middle line:90%
a slightly different game.

01:05:35.023 --> 01:05:37.190 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, here, we've kind of
made a simplification here

01:05:37.190 --> 01:05:40.630 align:middle line:84%
where we've basically
imagined that there's really

01:05:40.630 --> 01:05:41.990 align:middle line:90%
only one student.

01:05:41.990 --> 01:05:44.750 align:middle line:84%
So here what we're
imagining is a student

01:05:44.750 --> 01:05:49.630 align:middle line:84%
is drawn from the
population and applies.

01:05:49.630 --> 01:05:52.210 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a reasonable
model if-- in reality,

01:05:52.210 --> 01:05:53.770 align:middle line:84%
what happens is
many people apply,

01:05:53.770 --> 01:05:55.810 align:middle line:84%
but the school adopts
a uniform rule,

01:05:55.810 --> 01:05:58.962 align:middle line:84%
they either accept students with
essays or they don't, and they

01:05:58.962 --> 01:06:01.420 align:middle line:84%
either accept students who
don't have essays or they don't.

01:06:01.420 --> 01:06:03.740 align:middle line:84%
If they wanted to do something
more complicated where

01:06:03.740 --> 01:06:04.980 align:middle line:84%
there's five
applications and they

01:06:04.980 --> 01:06:06.480 align:middle line:84%
do different things to
different applications,

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:07.897 align:middle line:84%
then we'd have to
explicitly model

01:06:07.897 --> 01:06:09.160 align:middle line:90%
the number of applications.

01:06:09.160 --> 01:06:11.640 align:middle line:84%
And here, look at
the information set.

01:06:11.640 --> 01:06:14.700 align:middle line:84%
All I see is I see 1 applicant,
and it's either an essay or not

01:06:14.700 --> 01:06:15.380 align:middle line:90%
an essay.

01:06:15.380 --> 01:06:16.980 align:middle line:84%
And if we want to capture
what you're describing, which

01:06:16.980 --> 01:06:18.563 align:middle line:84%
I think is important
for applications,

01:06:18.563 --> 01:06:20.660 align:middle line:90%
we need to enrich the game.

01:06:20.660 --> 01:06:21.820 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:21.820 --> 01:06:23.653 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: We're just
looking at one student.

01:06:23.653 --> 01:06:26.620 align:middle line:84%
Or the interpretation
is a uniform policy that

01:06:26.620 --> 01:06:27.840 align:middle line:90%
treats all students equally.

01:06:27.840 --> 01:06:29.200 align:middle line:84%
If we wanted to treat
students differently,

01:06:29.200 --> 01:06:30.617 align:middle line:84%
then we'd have to
enrich the game.

01:06:30.617 --> 01:06:33.660 align:middle line:90%


01:06:33.660 --> 01:06:37.460 align:middle line:84%
Great, so this is
what's called a--

01:06:37.460 --> 01:06:39.060 align:middle line:90%
let's put this in words.

01:06:39.060 --> 01:06:41.480 align:middle line:84%
This is what's called
a pooling equilibrium.

01:06:41.480 --> 01:06:47.780 align:middle line:90%


01:06:47.780 --> 01:06:49.260 align:middle line:84%
It's called a
pooling equilibrium

01:06:49.260 --> 01:06:51.700 align:middle line:84%
because both types of
students, the passionate

01:06:51.700 --> 01:06:54.900 align:middle line:84%
and the non-passionate students,
choose the same action.

01:06:54.900 --> 01:06:57.740 align:middle line:84%
They pool together,
and all submit essays.

01:06:57.740 --> 01:07:00.580 align:middle line:84%
And as a result, their choice
about whether to submit an essay

01:07:00.580 --> 01:07:03.020 align:middle line:84%
doesn't reveal that
much information about

01:07:03.020 --> 01:07:05.140 align:middle line:84%
whether they're
passionate or not.

01:07:05.140 --> 01:07:07.300 align:middle line:84%
So next, let's see
if we can compute

01:07:07.300 --> 01:07:10.220 align:middle line:84%
what's called a separating
equilibrium where

01:07:10.220 --> 01:07:13.360 align:middle line:84%
different types of students
make different choices.

01:07:13.360 --> 01:07:18.860 align:middle line:90%


01:07:18.860 --> 01:07:20.260 align:middle line:90%
Let's go down here.

01:07:20.260 --> 01:07:21.760 align:middle line:84%
This should be the
last thing we do.

01:07:21.760 --> 01:07:36.620 align:middle line:90%


01:07:36.620 --> 01:07:38.440 align:middle line:84%
So earlier, I had
assumed that c--

01:07:38.440 --> 01:07:40.460 align:middle line:84%
remember c is the cost
of submitting an essay--

01:07:40.460 --> 01:07:41.680 align:middle line:90%
was less than 1.

01:07:41.680 --> 01:07:43.040 align:middle line:90%
Let's now relax that assumption.

01:07:43.040 --> 01:07:44.980 align:middle line:90%
And let's let c be arbitrary.

01:07:44.980 --> 01:07:48.480 align:middle line:90%
So let c be arbitrary.

01:07:48.480 --> 01:07:53.500 align:middle line:90%


01:07:53.500 --> 01:07:57.850 align:middle line:84%
And now what we'd like to do
is see, for which values of c,

01:07:57.850 --> 01:08:00.250 align:middle line:84%
can we find a
separating equilibrium?

01:08:00.250 --> 01:08:03.490 align:middle line:84%
And this is actually an exam
question I often like to ask,

01:08:03.490 --> 01:08:06.050 align:middle line:84%
where you try to find the
set of parameter values

01:08:06.050 --> 01:08:08.462 align:middle line:84%
such that something
is in equilibrium.

01:08:08.462 --> 01:08:10.170 align:middle line:84%
So let's see if we
can work through this.

01:08:10.170 --> 01:08:17.970 align:middle line:84%
So we want to look
for an equilibrium

01:08:17.970 --> 01:08:21.253 align:middle line:84%
where the essay distinguishes
the types of students.

01:08:21.253 --> 01:08:23.670 align:middle line:84%
I think this is exactly why
schools offer optional essays.

01:08:23.670 --> 01:08:25.370 align:middle line:84%
What they're hoping is that
people who really want to get in

01:08:25.370 --> 01:08:27.490 align:middle line:84%
and are excited write
the essay, and the people

01:08:27.490 --> 01:08:29.189 align:middle line:84%
who don't really want to get
in don't write the essay.

01:08:29.189 --> 01:08:30.609 align:middle line:84%
I think it often doesn't
work because we end up

01:08:30.609 --> 01:08:31.830 align:middle line:84%
in the other
equilibrium I described

01:08:31.830 --> 01:08:33.750 align:middle line:84%
where everyone submits the essay
even if they don't want to go.

01:08:33.750 --> 01:08:34.630 align:middle line:90%
But this is the idea.

01:08:34.630 --> 01:08:37.569 align:middle line:84%
And if essays are costly
enough, this is what might work.

01:08:37.569 --> 01:08:39.090 align:middle line:84%
So we're looking
for an equilibrium

01:08:39.090 --> 01:08:42.290 align:middle line:90%
where what is the student doing?

01:08:42.290 --> 01:08:49.010 align:middle line:84%
Well, if they're passionate,
they submit the essay.

01:08:49.010 --> 01:08:56.359 align:middle line:84%
And if they're not passionate,
they don't submit the essay.

01:08:56.359 --> 01:08:59.671 align:middle line:84%
And then what does
the school do?

01:08:59.671 --> 01:09:08.540 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's first specify
the school's beliefs.

01:09:08.540 --> 01:09:12.319 align:middle line:84%
In this case, both
information sets

01:09:12.319 --> 01:09:14.180 align:middle line:84%
are reached with
positive probability.

01:09:14.180 --> 01:09:15.640 align:middle line:84%
There's a positive
probability that a student

01:09:15.640 --> 01:09:17.457 align:middle line:84%
submits an essay and
a positive probability

01:09:17.457 --> 01:09:19.040 align:middle line:84%
that student does
not submit an essay.

01:09:19.040 --> 01:09:20.960 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, we
can apply Bayes' rule

01:09:20.960 --> 01:09:22.140 align:middle line:90%
at both information sets.

01:09:22.140 --> 01:09:24.200 align:middle line:84%
We don't run into this
issue of dividing by 0.

01:09:24.200 --> 01:09:26.158 align:middle line:84%
And these beliefs are
going to be well defined.

01:09:26.158 --> 01:09:28.840 align:middle line:84%
So if I see that a student
submits an essay given

01:09:28.840 --> 01:09:30.946 align:middle line:84%
that this is the strategy,
what does Bayes' rule

01:09:30.946 --> 01:09:31.779 align:middle line:90%
tell me as a school?

01:09:31.779 --> 01:09:33.140 align:middle line:84%
What is the probability
that they're passionate?

01:09:33.140 --> 01:09:35.859 align:middle line:84%
And what is the probability
that they're not passionate?

01:09:35.859 --> 01:09:36.359 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:09:36.359 --> 01:09:37.160 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 1?

01:09:37.160 --> 01:09:38.327 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah, it's just 1.

01:09:38.327 --> 01:09:40.840 align:middle line:84%
And I think the way I'm writing
it, let's write this 1, 0.

01:09:40.840 --> 01:09:42.298 align:middle line:84%
So I say there's
a probability of 1

01:09:42.298 --> 01:09:44.960 align:middle line:84%
that they're passionate and 0
that they're not passionate.

01:09:44.960 --> 01:09:46.620 align:middle line:84%
What if I see that
there's no essay?

01:09:46.620 --> 01:09:47.852 align:middle line:90%
Well, now it's the opposite.

01:09:47.852 --> 01:09:49.560 align:middle line:84%
The only students who
don't submit essays

01:09:49.560 --> 01:09:51.060 align:middle line:84%
are the ones who
are not passionate.

01:09:51.060 --> 01:09:53.319 align:middle line:84%
So I'm certain that they
must be not passionate.

01:09:53.319 --> 01:09:54.690 align:middle line:90%
And I get 0, 1.

01:09:54.690 --> 01:09:58.790 align:middle line:90%


01:09:58.790 --> 01:09:59.670 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:09:59.670 --> 01:10:01.313 align:middle line:84%
Well, now it's
pretty clear, if I'm

01:10:01.313 --> 01:10:03.730 align:middle line:84%
certain that students who
submit the essay are passionate,

01:10:03.730 --> 01:10:06.652 align:middle line:90%
I certainly want to accept them.

01:10:06.652 --> 01:10:09.110 align:middle line:84%
And if I'm certain that students
who don't submit the essay

01:10:09.110 --> 01:10:11.810 align:middle line:84%
are not passionate, then I
certainly want to reject them.

01:10:11.810 --> 01:10:19.530 align:middle line:90%


01:10:19.530 --> 01:10:23.310 align:middle line:84%
So here's our
candidate equilibrium.

01:10:23.310 --> 01:10:27.230 align:middle line:84%
And let's just go
through the conditions.

01:10:27.230 --> 01:10:29.450 align:middle line:84%
And I think this is always
a good way to check.

01:10:29.450 --> 01:10:42.870 align:middle line:90%


01:10:42.870 --> 01:10:46.390 align:middle line:84%
So does this equilibrium
satisfy belief consistency?

01:10:46.390 --> 01:10:47.210 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:10:47.210 --> 01:10:50.830 align:middle line:84%
We just checked Bayes'
rule, so we're good here.

01:10:50.830 --> 01:10:52.890 align:middle line:84%
And just to remind you,
when I say equilibrium,

01:10:52.890 --> 01:10:55.130 align:middle line:84%
notice I've not just
specified the strategies.

01:10:55.130 --> 01:10:57.310 align:middle line:90%
I've also specified the beliefs.

01:10:57.310 --> 01:10:59.470 align:middle line:84%
And that's because this
is an assessment, not just

01:10:59.470 --> 01:11:01.910 align:middle line:90%
a strategy profile.

01:11:01.910 --> 01:11:03.510 align:middle line:84%
And so I have
belief consistency.

01:11:03.510 --> 01:11:05.303 align:middle line:84%
Now we move to
sequential rationality.

01:11:05.303 --> 01:11:07.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me separately check it
for the student and the school.

01:11:07.970 --> 01:11:15.430 align:middle line:90%


01:11:15.430 --> 01:11:17.730 align:middle line:84%
Sequential rationality for
the school is pretty clear.

01:11:17.730 --> 01:11:18.570 align:middle line:90%
This is what I already said.

01:11:18.570 --> 01:11:19.670 align:middle line:84%
They're accepting
students who they

01:11:19.670 --> 01:11:21.090 align:middle line:84%
think are likely
to be passionate,

01:11:21.090 --> 01:11:22.548 align:middle line:84%
and they're rejecting
students they

01:11:22.548 --> 01:11:24.470 align:middle line:84%
think are likely to
be not passionate.

01:11:24.470 --> 01:11:26.880 align:middle line:90%
So we're good here.

01:11:26.880 --> 01:11:29.130 align:middle line:84%
But what about sequential
rationality for the student,

01:11:29.130 --> 01:11:30.450 align:middle line:84%
this is going to be
a little trickier.

01:11:30.450 --> 01:11:31.825 align:middle line:84%
And this is going
to depend on c.

01:11:31.825 --> 01:11:32.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:11:32.390 --> 01:11:34.307 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE]
the expected value

01:11:34.307 --> 01:11:39.652 align:middle line:84%
for passionate students have to
be like, negative [INAUDIBLE].

01:11:39.652 --> 01:11:41.610 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right, so let's
exactly check it out.

01:11:41.610 --> 01:11:43.030 align:middle line:84%
So there's two things
we have to check.

01:11:43.030 --> 01:11:45.230 align:middle line:84%
We have to check that if
I'm a passionate student,

01:11:45.230 --> 01:11:47.330 align:middle line:84%
I prefer writing
an essay to not.

01:11:47.330 --> 01:11:48.830 align:middle line:84%
And if I'm a
not-passionate student,

01:11:48.830 --> 01:11:51.020 align:middle line:84%
I prefer not writing an
essay to writing an essay.

01:11:51.020 --> 01:11:53.360 align:middle line:84%
So let's just write that
down in math, but in words,

01:11:53.360 --> 01:11:55.220 align:middle line:90%
that's exactly what we want.

01:11:55.220 --> 01:11:58.780 align:middle line:84%
So let's suppose I'm a
student who's passionate.

01:11:58.780 --> 01:12:03.918 align:middle line:84%
And if I write an essay,
what is my payoff?

01:12:03.918 --> 01:12:05.960 align:middle line:84%
And if I don't write an
essay, what is my payoff?

01:12:05.960 --> 01:12:09.940 align:middle line:90%


01:12:09.940 --> 01:12:13.640 align:middle line:84%
Well, if I'm passionate and I
write an essay, what happens?

01:12:13.640 --> 01:12:14.680 align:middle line:90%
What's my payoff here?

01:12:14.680 --> 01:12:18.580 align:middle line:90%


01:12:18.580 --> 01:12:20.200 align:middle line:90%
2 minus c.

01:12:20.200 --> 01:12:23.348 align:middle line:90%


01:12:23.348 --> 01:12:24.140 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:12:24.140 --> 01:12:28.580 align:middle line:84%
And if I don't write an
essay, well, I'm not accepted,

01:12:28.580 --> 01:12:31.040 align:middle line:84%
and I don't write the essay,
so my payoff is just 0.

01:12:31.040 --> 01:12:33.920 align:middle line:90%


01:12:33.920 --> 01:12:37.660 align:middle line:84%
Now, notice a really
crucial point here.

01:12:37.660 --> 01:12:41.340 align:middle line:84%
The school's strategy is that
if you don't submit an essay,

01:12:41.340 --> 01:12:43.380 align:middle line:90%
you're rejected.

01:12:43.380 --> 01:12:46.660 align:middle line:84%
When I, as a passionate student,
contemplate deviating and not

01:12:46.660 --> 01:12:49.490 align:middle line:84%
submitting an essay, when
I don't submit an essay,

01:12:49.490 --> 01:12:52.182 align:middle line:84%
the school's strategy is fixed,
and therefore, they reject me.

01:12:52.182 --> 01:12:54.390 align:middle line:84%
People often get confused
here because you might say,

01:12:54.390 --> 01:12:55.870 align:middle line:84%
well, the school's
belief is wrong.

01:12:55.870 --> 01:12:58.690 align:middle line:84%
Their belief is that only
non-passionate students

01:12:58.690 --> 01:13:00.130 align:middle line:90%
don't submit essays.

01:13:00.130 --> 01:13:02.462 align:middle line:84%
And now a passionate
student's submitting an essay.

01:13:02.462 --> 01:13:04.170 align:middle line:84%
But the school can't
tell the difference.

01:13:04.170 --> 01:13:07.270 align:middle line:84%
If the school's belief is that
if you don't submit an essay,

01:13:07.270 --> 01:13:08.690 align:middle line:90%
you can't be passionate.

01:13:08.690 --> 01:13:11.130 align:middle line:84%
And if a passionate student
deviates and doesn't

01:13:11.130 --> 01:13:13.410 align:middle line:84%
send an essay, they're not
able to change the beliefs

01:13:13.410 --> 01:13:14.487 align:middle line:90%
that the school has.

01:13:14.487 --> 01:13:16.570 align:middle line:84%
The school has their beliefs
fixed, and therefore,

01:13:16.570 --> 01:13:18.090 align:middle line:84%
what happens to
them when they don't

01:13:18.090 --> 01:13:21.000 align:middle line:84%
write an essay is determined
by the strategy of the school.

01:13:21.000 --> 01:13:21.750 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't change.

01:13:21.750 --> 01:13:23.510 align:middle line:84%
Some people are tempted to
say, oh, they're passionate,

01:13:23.510 --> 01:13:25.218 align:middle line:84%
so now the school's
going to accept them.

01:13:25.218 --> 01:13:26.770 align:middle line:90%
No, it's a unilateral deviation.

01:13:26.770 --> 01:13:28.390 align:middle line:90%
The school strategy is fixed.

01:13:28.390 --> 01:13:29.807 align:middle line:84%
And we're considering
what happens

01:13:29.807 --> 01:13:32.290 align:middle line:84%
when a passionate student
deviates by not submitting this.

01:13:32.290 --> 01:13:35.090 align:middle line:84%
OK, now let's consider a
non-passionate student.

01:13:35.090 --> 01:13:37.890 align:middle line:90%
They're not submitting an essay.

01:13:37.890 --> 01:13:40.570 align:middle line:84%
And again, they're
getting a payoff of 0.

01:13:40.570 --> 01:13:42.070 align:middle line:90%
What if they submitted an essay?

01:13:42.070 --> 01:13:44.970 align:middle line:90%


01:13:44.970 --> 01:13:47.510 align:middle line:90%
What would their payoff be?

01:13:47.510 --> 01:13:48.920 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:13:48.920 --> 01:13:53.538 align:middle line:84%
1 minus C. Again, they're
able to trick the school.

01:13:53.538 --> 01:13:56.080 align:middle line:84%
If they submit the essay, they
successfully trick the school,

01:13:56.080 --> 01:13:57.620 align:middle line:84%
and the school's
going to accept them

01:13:57.620 --> 01:13:58.520 align:middle line:84%
even though the
school doesn't want

01:13:58.520 --> 01:14:00.640 align:middle line:84%
to accept non-passionate
people, but they have

01:14:00.640 --> 01:14:02.280 align:middle line:90%
to pay the essay cost of c.

01:14:02.280 --> 01:14:04.920 align:middle line:84%
So now let's check the
equilibrium conditions.

01:14:04.920 --> 01:14:07.400 align:middle line:84%
A passionate student is
supposed to submit the essay,

01:14:07.400 --> 01:14:11.680 align:middle line:84%
so we need 2 minus c to be
greater than or equal to 0.

01:14:11.680 --> 01:14:13.400 align:middle line:84%
But a non-passionate
student is not

01:14:13.400 --> 01:14:14.960 align:middle line:90%
supposed to submit the essay.

01:14:14.960 --> 01:14:20.280 align:middle line:84%
So we need 0 greater than
or equal to 1 minus c.

01:14:20.280 --> 01:14:24.440 align:middle line:90%
So what's our solution?

01:14:24.440 --> 01:14:26.840 align:middle line:84%
Well, this is going
to be an equilibrium

01:14:26.840 --> 01:14:30.760 align:middle line:84%
if both of these
inequalities are satisfied.

01:14:30.760 --> 01:14:35.080 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to be true if
1 is less than or equal to c

01:14:35.080 --> 01:14:37.465 align:middle line:90%
less than or equal to 2.

01:14:37.465 --> 01:14:39.340 align:middle line:84%
And let's try to understand
the role of this.

01:14:39.340 --> 01:14:40.960 align:middle line:84%
Why do we have these
two assumptions?

01:14:40.960 --> 01:14:44.160 align:middle line:84%
Well, we want the essay to
separate the good students

01:14:44.160 --> 01:14:47.000 align:middle line:90%
from the bad students.

01:14:47.000 --> 01:14:49.060 align:middle line:84%
What would go wrong
if c was too big?

01:14:49.060 --> 01:14:50.680 align:middle line:90%
If c was greater than 2?

01:14:50.680 --> 01:14:51.200 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:14:51.200 --> 01:14:52.835 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Even the
good students--

01:14:52.835 --> 01:14:54.460 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yeah, it's
so costly-- if you

01:14:54.460 --> 01:14:55.660 align:middle line:84%
say you have to write
100 essays, well,

01:14:55.660 --> 01:14:57.327 align:middle line:84%
no one's going to
apply to your school--

01:14:57.327 --> 01:14:58.520 align:middle line:90%
again, in the pre-GPT world.

01:14:58.520 --> 01:15:00.312 align:middle line:84%
So we're not able to
separate the students.

01:15:00.312 --> 01:15:03.120 align:middle line:84%
If c is too low,
what goes wrong?

01:15:03.120 --> 01:15:06.180 align:middle line:84%
If c is less than 1, well, even
the non-passionate students say,

01:15:06.180 --> 01:15:08.480 align:middle line:84%
well, it's a one-paragraph
essay, I can do that.

01:15:08.480 --> 01:15:10.080 align:middle line:90%
It's worth it for me to do that.

01:15:10.080 --> 01:15:12.080 align:middle line:84%
So if you think of
the school as-- this

01:15:12.080 --> 01:15:13.622 align:middle line:84%
is a broader question
about the game,

01:15:13.622 --> 01:15:15.247 align:middle line:84%
you could think of
the design question,

01:15:15.247 --> 01:15:17.800 align:middle line:84%
if the school can choose c by
choosing how hard the essay is,

01:15:17.800 --> 01:15:19.258 align:middle line:84%
they might want to
choose the essay

01:15:19.258 --> 01:15:20.660 align:middle line:90%
to be a moderate difficulty.

01:15:20.660 --> 01:15:23.480 align:middle line:84%
It's hard enough to deter
the uninspired students

01:15:23.480 --> 01:15:26.520 align:middle line:84%
from applying, but easy enough
that the inspired students would

01:15:26.520 --> 01:15:27.280 align:middle line:90%
still apply.

01:15:27.280 --> 01:15:29.920 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's exactly
what admissions offices

01:15:29.920 --> 01:15:32.840 align:middle line:90%
and jobs think about.

01:15:32.840 --> 01:15:36.200 align:middle line:84%
OK, let me stop there unless
there's any questions.

01:15:36.200 --> 01:15:39.070 align:middle line:90%
And I'll see everyone next week.

01:15:39.070 --> 01:15:44.000 align:middle line:90%